{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"endpoint-stop! appears to conform to the spec. One subtle point: `invoke-close!` can throw an exception / return :failed — in WsHandle's close!, a close failure emits `:ws/error :ws/close-failure`. In endpoint-stop!, the result of invoke-close! is ignored. Let me check invoke-close!.\n\nAlso regarding ordering: `swap! hstate assoc :phase :closed` followed by invoke-close, then terminal!. terminal! emits `:ws/closed` — but deposit! checks the phase... no wait, deposit! is unrelated to the phase. Actually, terminal! uses emit! which uses deposit!, appending to the control medium via the handle. No problem.\n\nOne concern: what about slots that have status :reserving? If accept-connection! claimed a slot (:reserving) and then the offer append failed, it should have released the slot. But in a reentrant/concurrent scenario... In a single-threaded stepped system, accept-connection! runs synchronously, so at the point where endpoint-stop! is called between callbacks, :reserving should never remain. However, it's possible that the host calls accept-connection! from a different thread. Probably out of scope.\n\nLet me check invoke-close!.\n"}}
