{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"These look solid. Next, verify the fixture change in head_ws_test.cljc (currently uses the moved loopback-net) and check for regressions. Also review doc changes. And think about defects in remote_channel.cljc.\n\nKey adversarial checks so far:\n\n1. `serve`'s bind-failed path: `(refused (select-keys server [:spec :descriptor]) ::bind-failed {})` — on bind failure the endpoint and acceptor are dropped, but pending connections can't exist yet (nothing has been bound... actually bind! might partially succeed). Spec §4.1 said \"accept-connection! works as long as the host listener isn't gone afterwards.\" When bind fails, could the endpoint have accepted a connection? No — accept! is only called via the listener. But if `bind!` throws mid-way, could the listener be partially registered? The lifecycle-lost path calls endpoint-stop! best-effort; the bind-failed path does not. The spec says \"if `bind!` throws, returns non-ok, or deposits `:bind-failed`, then `::bind-failed`.\" The spec doesn't require endpoint-stop there. Minor.\n\n2. `serve-step`'s :starting/:serving path: after drain-lifecycle, if status is still starting/serving, accept-step!. Good — avoids stepping a refused/stopped acceptor.\n\n3. `begin-stop`: runs accept-step! once, then release!, then unbind!. Spec §4.3 ordering: 1. accept-step!, 2. close-sessions! then endpoint-stop!, 3. unbind!. release! runs ws-project/stop!, close-sessions!, endpoint-stop!. Matches. If unbind fails → stopped with ::unbind-failed. Good.\n\nWait — subtle issue in begin-stop: `accept-step!` also internally runs `ws/endpoint-step`, which consumes acks. Fine.\n\nBut: `begin-stop` runs accept-step!, which runs the last restricted answering pass. But close-sessions! runs after the mirror pass inside accept-step!? Actually accept-step! does: endpoint-step, adopt offers, endpoint-step, per-session projection+mirror, reap. Then release! closes sessions. So the \"last restricted answering pass\" happens before closing. Good, matches spec.\n\n4. `continue-stop`: runs accept-step! + endpoint-stop! each tick, then drain-lifecycle, then grace check. Engineer's deviation #4 — justified.\n\nIssue: `continue-stop`'s grace check uses `(get-in server [:stop :since])`. If status is still :stopping, since is guaranteed non-nil. OK.\n\n5. `drain-lifecycle`'s :stopping status: observe handles `:stopped` kind during :stopping → stopped :confirmed. Good. `lifecycle-lost` during :stopping → ::unconfirmed stopped. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle bug in observe: `(= :stopped kind)` catch-all — if during :starting a :stopped fact arrives, becomes release! + stopped ::host-stopped (deviation #5 — spec left this unspecified, reasonable). But `stopped` does `(assoc (:stop server) :outcome outcome)` — if `:stop` is nil (server never called stop!), `(assoc nil :outcome ...)` → {:outcome ...}. Then in stop-is-explicit... host-stopped test asserts `(get-in @(:server w) [:stop :outcome])` = ::host-stopped. `:since` missing. Minor, acceptable.\n\nWait, but assoc on nil: `(::outcome nil)`... `(assoc nil :outcome x)` works in Clojure → {:outcome x}. In CLJS? `(assoc nil :a 1)` → {:a 1}, works. In CLJD (Dart)? Probably compiles to same semantics. OK, but the engineer noted the CLJD trap \"multi-key assoc on nil.\" Here it's single-key assoc on nil, fine.\n\n6. `release!` — for host-stopped during :serving, calls ws-project/stop! first (deviation #5), then close-sessions!, then endpoint-stop!. But doesn't call unbind!. Hmm — if host stopped under us, the listener is already gone (host deposited :stopped). If :stopped fact came from unlisten! but the listener is still registered... net's unlisten! dissocs the listener first. Fine. But what about a host that deposits :stopped without removing its own listener? Then no unbind — acceptable, host said stopped.\n\n7. `lifecycle-lost`'s :starting case: `(unbind! server)` then endpoint-stop!, refused. But the `refused` server retains the :endpoint and :acceptor keys (refused merges into server). Fine.\n\nBut wait: `lifecycle-lost`'s :starting — unbind! returns a bool that's ignored; fine, best-effort as spec says.\n\n8. `dial` — passes `:table {}`, no `:names`. Spec says dial composes ws-project/dial with 3 step bounds and 7 policy keys. Dial's own mirror with empty table: dial-side mirror-step answers requests from the other side with nothing — that's fine for a pure reader.\n\n9. `dial-step`'s :resolving — calls `resolve-step` every tick. resolve-step calls `dial-resolve!`, which on first call establishes the channel (attach!). Retries until ok. Spec: \":resolving: dial-resolve! against :name; ok → dial-reflect!...\" matches.\n\nOne issue: resolve-step's attach failure — `dial-resolve!` may return attach!'s outcome (non-ok), e.g. transport-error. Then cond falls through to :else → status :lost with that outcome. But if attach transiently fails (e.g., connect busy)? The original head.ws/dial-step probably handled this similarly. Spec says \"transport-error with :dao.stream/retry? true → stay.\" If attach fails without retry?, then :lost. Consistent with spec.\n\n10. `channel-lost?` reads `(:gone? ch)` and projection closed?. Deviation: :gone? flag added in ws_project's dial-step!. Note: `channel-lost?` on a dial with no channel (nil ch): `(:gone? nil)` → nil, `(some-> (:project nil) ...)` → nil. So nil → not lost. Fine.\n\nBut a subtle ordering point in `dial-step`: it calls `(ws-project/dial-step! (:dial d) now)`, then checks status. During :resolving, resolve-step is called after dial-step!; dial-step! steps projection and link. Fine.\n\nQuestion: during :attached, when the link expires (channel-gone), ws_project's dial-step! closes the ws handle and sets :gone?. Then remote-channel's dial-step marks :lost with channel-gone outcome. Good — this is the §5.2 \"traffic medium gap evicted :ws/closed\" line.\n\n11. `close!` — closes ws handle via stream/close! a