t 4, but (last \"127.0.0.1.\") is \\. → false. Good, docstring says so.\n\noctet? checks 1-3 digits and ≤255, but also allows leading zeros (\"127.000.000.001\" → loopback true). Does a resolver treat leading zeros as octal? Irrelevant, conservative direction? Actually 127.000.0.1 is still loopback. Leading-zero quads like \"127.0.0.01\" are fine. Then \"0177.0.0.1\" (octal for 127.0.0.1)? first part \"0177\" ≠ \"127\" → false → refuses serve for what might be loopback. Conservative = safe. Fine.\n\nCompare to old head.ws/loopback? — probably same logic. Let me check that behavior doesn't regress (moved function). Let me look at head/ws.cljc's loopback?.\n\n28. Test `production-bounds-reach-every-layer` asserts `(= 20 (count p))`. Fine per deviation #1 — spec table indeed has 20 rows. Good judgment.\n\n29. remote_channel_test's stop test: asserts `(every? :closed? (vals (rc/sessions ...)))` — sessions after first stopping tick marked closed. Then after second tick, sessions empty.\n\n30. `a-newcomer-during-stop-is-rejected` — dial after stop!; run-until ticks server (stopping path: accept-step! rejects offer, closes handle, client side gets closed → projection closes ring → resolve answers channel-gone transport-error non-retry → :lost). Deviation #6 explains the outcome differs from spec §7's :ws/transport-error. Spec said \"resolve returns :ws/transport-error\" — dial outcome is transport-error. Test asserts outcome's :dao.stream/outcome is transport-error. Consistent enough.\n\nNext check ws_project.cljc's remaining parts: make-acceptor (table validation), close-session-resources!, reaped, projection, step!, closed?. And remote.cljc's links for policy validation and events usage. Plus yin/repl/host.cljc's bind! shape and head/ws.cljc's loopback? for comparison. And doc diffs.\n\nLet me read ws_project.cljc lines 1-300.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the"}]}}
