{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"These look solid. Next, verify the fixture change in head_ws_test.cljc (currently uses the moved loopback-net) and check for regressions. Also review doc changes. And think about defects in remote_channel.cljc.\n\nKey adversarial checks so far:\n\n1. `serve`'s bind-failed path: `(refused (select-keys server [:spec :descriptor]) ::bind-failed {})` — on bind failure the endpoint and acceptor are dropped, but pending connections can't exist yet (nothing has been bound... actually bind! might partially succeed). Spec §4.1 said \"accept-connection! works as long as the host listener isn't gone afterwards.\" When bind fails, could the endpoint have accepted a connection? No — accept! is only called via the listener. But if `bind!` throws mid-way, could the listener be partially registered? The lifecycle-lost path calls endpoint-stop! best-effort; the bind-failed path does not. The spec says \"if `bind!` throws, returns non-ok, or deposits `:bind-failed`, then `::bind-failed`.\" The spec doesn't require endpoint-stop there. Minor.\n\n2. `serve-step`'s :starting/:serving path: after drain-lifecycle, if status is still starting/serving, accept-step!. Good — avoids stepping a refused/stopped acceptor.\n\n3. `begin-stop`: runs accept-step! once, then release!, then unbind!. Spec §4.3 ordering: 1. accept-step!, 2. close-sessions! then endpoint-stop!, 3. unbind!. release! runs ws-project/stop!, close-sessions!, endpoint-stop!. Matches. If unbind fails → stopped with ::unbind-failed. Good.\n\nWait — subtle issue in begin-stop: `accept-step!` also internally runs `ws/endpoint-step`, which consumes acks. Fine.\n\nBut: `begin-stop` runs accept-step!, which runs the last restricted answering pass. But close-sessions! runs after the mirror pass inside accept-step!? Actually accept-step! does: endpoint-step, adopt offers, endpoint-step, per-session projection+mirror, reap. Then release! closes sessions. So the \"last restricted answering pass\" happens before closing. Good, matches spec.\n\n4. `continue-stop`: runs accept-step! + endpoint-stop! each tick, then drain-lifecycle, then grace check. Engineer's deviation #4 — justified.\n\nIssue: `continue-stop`'s grace check uses `(get-in server [:stop :since])`. If status is still :stopping, since is guaranteed non-nil. OK.\n\n5. `drain-lifecycle`'s :stopping status: observe handles `:stopped` kind during :stopping → stopped :confirmed. Good. `lifecycle-lost` during :stopping → ::unconfirmed stopped. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle bug in observe: `(= :stopped kind)` catch-all — if during :starting a :stopped fact arrives, becomes release! + stopped ::host-stopped (deviation #5 — spec left this unspecified, reasonable). But `stopped` does `(assoc (:stop server) :outcome outcome)` — if `:stop` is nil (server never called stop!), `(assoc nil :outcome ...)` → {:outcome ...}. Then in stop-is-explicit... host-stopped test asserts `(get-in @(:server w) [:stop :outcome])` = ::host-stopped. `:since` missing. Minor, acceptable.\n\nWait, but assoc on nil: `(::outcome nil)`... `(assoc nil :outcome x)` works in Clojure → {:outcome x}. In CLJS? `(assoc nil :a 1)` → {:a 1}, works. In CLJD (Dart)? Probably compiles to same semantics. OK, but the engineer noted the CLJD trap \"multi-key assoc on nil.\" Here it's single-key assoc on nil, fine.\n\n6. `release!` — for host-stopped during :serving, calls ws-project/stop! first (deviation #5), then close-sessions!, then endpoint-stop!. But doesn't call unbind!. Hmm — if host stopped under us, the listener is already gone (host deposited :stopped). If :stopped fact came from unlisten! but the listener is still registered... net's unlisten! dissocs the listener first. Fine. But what about a host that deposits :stopped without removing its own listener? Then no unbind — acceptable, host said stopped.\n\n7. `lifecycle-lost`'s :starting case: `(unbind! server)` then endpoint-stop!, refused. But the `refused` server retains the :endpoint and :acceptor keys (refused merges into server). Fine.\n\nBut wait: `lifecycle-lost`'s :starting — unbind! returns a bool that's ignored; fine, best-effort as spec says.\n\n8. `dial` — passes `:table {}`, no `:names`. Spec says dial composes ws-project/dial with 3 step bounds and 7 policy keys. Dial's own mirror with empty table: dial-side mirror-step answers requests from the other side with nothing — that's fine for a pure reader.\n\n9. `dial-step`'s :resolving — calls `resolve-step` every tick. resolve-step calls `dial-resolve!`, which on first call establishes the channel (attach!). Retries until ok. Spec: \":resolving: dial-resolve! against :name; ok → dial-reflect!...\" matches.\n\nOne issue: resolve-step's attach failure — `dial-resolve!` may return attach!'s outcome (non-ok), e.g. transport-error. Then cond falls through to :else → status :lost with that outcome. But if attach transiently fails (e.g., connect busy)? The original head.ws/dial-step probably handled this similarly. Spec says \"transport-error with :dao.stream/retry? true → stay.\" If attach fails without retry?, then :lost. Consistent with spec.\n\n10. `channel-lost?` reads `(:gone? ch)` and projection closed?. Deviation: :gone? flag added in ws_project's dial-step!. Note: `channel-lost?` on a dial with no channel (nil ch): `(:gone? nil)` → nil, `(some-> (:project nil) ...)` → nil. So nil → not lost. Fine.\n\nBut a subtle ordering point in `dial-step`: it calls `(ws-project/dial-step! (:dial d) now)`, then checks status. During :resolving, resolve-step is called after dial-step!; dial-step! steps projection and link. Fine.\n\nQuestion: during :attached, when the link expires (channel-gone), ws_project's dial-step! closes the ws handle and sets :gone?. Then remote-channel's dial-step marks :lost with channel-gone outcome. Good — this is the §5.2 \"traffic medium gap evicted :ws/closed\" line.\n\n11. `close!` — closes ws handle via stream/close! and reflection handle if closable. Idempotent for :closed. But if close! is called on :lost dial, ws handle is already closed; stream/close! on a closed handle → returns closed outcome, harmless.\n\n12. endpoint-stop! — for pending slots: swap phase :closed, invoke-close! 1001, terminal! :ws/closed, release-slot!. Note ordering: invoke-close! before terminal!. In loopback-net, close! enqueues a callback that calls both sides' closed!, which could synchronously deposit :ws/closed via closed!... but pump is deferred, fine. On real host (http-kit), close! could trigger callback synchronously; handle's closed! would call terminal! itself; then endpoint-stop's terminal! is a no-op (terminal? already set). Actually closed! → let me check ws's closed!. Sets terminal. Then invoke-close returns; terminal! finds :terminal? true, doesn't emit. Then release-slot!. Fine, exactly-once.\n\nWhat if `invoke-close!` fails (returns :failed because host close threw)? WsHandle's close! path emits :ws/error :ws/close-failure diagnostic. endpoint-stop! ignores the failure — no diagnostic. Minor: spec §4.1 doesn't require diagnostics. Acceptable, though could note as nit.\n\n13. Slot clearing: endpoint-stop! iterates slots in state snapshot; release-slot! per slot. Race: host could call accept-connection! during doseq (single-threaded driver — no). Slots with status :reserving not closed by endpoint-stop! — :reserving is only transient within accept-connection!. OK.\n\n14. `adopt!` stopping rejection: check `(not (:stopping? @acceptor))` in condition; when stopping, offered handle is closed via stream/close! (generic close, 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\"). Spec §4.2: \"offers are rejected as with cap: offered handle closed, no ack, no media.\" Cap rejection also uses stream/close!. Engineer's deviation #3 explains that pending connections at stop time are closed by adopt!'s rejection with 1000 detached, not endpoint-stop!'s 1001. Spec §4.3 says first tick runs accept-step!, then endpoint-stop!. Hmm — is 1000 \"detached\" the right close code for a rejected newcomer during stop? For cap rejection, ws.md probably assigns that. During stop, one could argue 1001 (going away) is semantically better, but spec ordering (accept-step! first) means adopt!'s rejection path runs first, so this matches the spec's own §4.3 sequence. Consistent.\n\nBut wait — real subtlety: accept-step! during stopping runs endpoint-step twice, consuming acks. If a connection was already acknowledged by acceptor pre-stop... no, adopt! rejects before ack.\n\nAnother subtlety: adopt! rejects by closing handle but doesn't release slot — endpoint-step releases pending slots whose handle-state phase is :closed (first doseq). stream/close! sets phase to :closed, so next endpoint-step releases the slot. accept-step! runs endpoint-step at start and after adoption, so slot released same tick. Fine.\n\n15. close-sessions! — iterates @acceptor's sessions, closes resources, marks closed?. If session was added concurrently... driver-only. Reap next tick. OK.\n\nNote: close-sessions! is \"outside swap!, driver-only, like reap-sessions!\" — matches.\n\n16. Invariants: no ambient scheduler — remote-channel only reads driver's `now`. production-bounds is data. No new core stream ops — uses stream/append!, next, cursor, close!. Transport neutrality — yin unchanged in S3a-1 (head.ws still has its own loopback? — deviation #9, fine since S3a-2 removes it; spec's §8 S3a-1 row only covers ws.cljc, ws_project.cljc, remote_channel.cljc, tests, docs — correct).\n\nHmm wait — one more: spec §2.1 says `serve` refuses with `::invalid-table` when \"any table entry's cursor holds a host object, i.e., make-acceptor throws.\" Engineer's deviation #2 changed this to structural check. Because make-acceptor throw also indicates bad bounds. Is make-acceptor validating table cursors? Let me check make-acceptor to see what it validates about tables. Also whether table with :handle that isn't a valid stream gets caught. Structural check `(some? (:handle e))` only requires handle presence, not that it's a reader. If handle is a non-stream object, make-acceptor may not throw, and later mirror-step fails or throws, isolated per-session try/catch → session closed. Hmm. Let me check make-acceptor.\n\n17. `serve` validation ordering: spec says \"serve passes bounds to make-endpoint, make-acceptor, links for validation; composition errors from any of these are re-thrown before anything is listened to.\" Engineer calls `(link-policy b)` before lifecycle/bind. make-endpoint and make-acceptor called earlier. Test asserts nothing was listened to on bad bounds. Good.\n\nBut: `make-slots` runs before `make-endpoint`; if make-endpoint throws, the ring is already allocated — harmless garbage.\n\n18. `serve`'s bind! invocation: passes a map with :endpoint, :bind-host, :bind-port, :path, :ws/max-frame-bytes, :accept!, :deposit!. Check what yin.repl.host/websocket's bind expects — for S3a-2 compatibility. head.ws currently uses host's (:bind! node) with what arg shape? S3a-1 has no consumers yet, so shape is remote-channel's own contract. But the spec says host ::= yin.repl.host's seam shape. Let me check yin/repl/host.cljc's bind! signature to verify compat. If shape differs, S3a-2 will need adaptation. Worth checking.\n\n19. `ok-or-nil?` — treats non-map responses as ok. If bind! returns nil → ok. Fine.\n\n20. lifecycle `observe` during :starting on :bind-succeeded → :serving. But drain-lifecycle only runs during serve-step. Spec §5.1. Good.\n\nAlso `drain-lifecycle` stops draining when status leaves live (e.g., refused). Facts remaining in medium. Fine.\n\n21. `continue-stop` calls accept-step! every stopping tick — this also runs reap-sessions!, so sessions eventually get cleared. Test asserts sessions empty after confirmed stop. Good.\n\n22. Potential defect: in `serve-step`'s :starting/:serving, if server became :refused via observe (bind-failed fact), accept-step! is skipped due to status check. But endpoint's pending slots aren't closed. Spec §5.1 for bind-failed during :starting says refuse — doesn't require endpoint-stop (spec only lists unbind + endpoint-stop for lifecycle-lost). Hmm, actually for bind-failed, spec table just says \"refused bind-failed.\" Pending connections can't exist if bind failed... unless the host accepted upgrades before depositing bind-failed. Edge case; spec didn't require. Fine.\n\n23. `stop!` on :starting server: sets :stopping, calls ws-project/stop!. First stopping tick of serve-step runs accept-step! → releases. But if lifecycle never got :bind-succeeded, begin-stop unbinds. OK.\n\n24. `stopped` helper: `(assoc (:stop server) :outcome outcome)` — for confirmed stop, :since preserved. For host-stopped (no :stop), produces {:outcome ...} without :since. Spec says `:stop` (`{:since now :outcome o}` once begun) — host-stopped never began; the map shape difference is acceptable.\n\n25. remote-channel's dial: `(when events {:dao.stream.remote/events events})` — events is writer-target or handle? In the test, `events` is `(ring 64)` — a raw handle. And ws-project/dial selects :dao.stream.remote/events and passes to remote/links' policy. Need to check what remote/links expects for events — in remote.cljc, does `emit!` write with stream/append! to (:dao.stream.remote/events policy)? Let me verify.\n\n26. Check `descriptor-of`: identity \"ws://host:port path\". ws/descriptor? validates. Fine.\n\n27. `loopback-literal?`: `(not= \\. (last host))` — `last` on empty string returns nil; but `(count parts) = 4` fails for \"\". Order: contains? ipv6 first, then split. For \"\" → parts is [\"\"]? Actually (str/split \"\" #\"\\.\") → []? In Clojure (str/split \"\" #\"\\.\") returns []. Then `(not= \\. (last \"\"))` → last of \"\" is nil, not= \\. nil → true. (= 4 (count [])) → false. OK, returns false. For nil host: string? is false → nil/false. Good.\n\n\"127.0.0.1.\" → parts (split drops trailing empties) [\"127\" \"0\" \"0\" \"1\"] count 4, but (last \"127.0.0.1.\") is \\. → false. Good, docstring says so.\n\noctet? checks 1-3 digits and ≤255, but also allows leading zeros (\"127.000.000.001\" → loopback true). Does a resolver treat leading zeros as octal? Irrelevant, conservative direction? Actually 127.000.0.1 is still loopback. Leading-zero quads like \"127.0.0.01\" are fine. Then \"0177.0.0.1\" (octal for 127.0.0.1)? first part \"0177\" ≠ \"127\" → false → refuses serve for what might be loopback. Conservative = safe. Fine.\n\nCompare to old head.ws/loopback? — probably same logic. Let me check that behavior doesn't regress (moved function). Let me look at head/ws.cljc's loopback?.\n\n28. Test `production-bounds-reach-every-layer` asserts `(= 20 (count p))`. Fine per deviation #1 — spec table indeed has 20 rows. Good judgment.\n\n29. remote_channel_test's stop test: asserts `(every? :closed? (vals (rc/sessions ...)))` — sessions after first stopping tick marked closed. Then after second tick, sessions empty.\n\n30. `a-newcomer-during-stop-is-rejected` — dial after stop!; run-until ticks server (stopping path: accept-step! rejects offer, closes handle, client side gets closed → projection closes ring → resolve answers channel-gone transport-error non-retry → :lost). Deviation #6 explains the outcome differs from spec §7's :ws/transport-error. Spec said \"resolve returns :ws/transport-error\" — dial outcome is transport-error. Test asserts outcome's :dao.stream/outcome is transport-error. Consistent enough.\n\nNext check ws_project.cljc's remaining parts: make-acceptor (table validation), close-session-resources!, reaped, projection, step!, closed?. And remote.cljc's links for policy validation and events usage. Plus yin/repl/host.cljc's bind! shape and head/ws.cljc's loopback? for comparison. And doc diffs.\n\nLet me read ws_project.cljc lines 1-300.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the rest of ws_project"}]}}
