{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"loopback-literal? was copied as-is from head.ws/loopback? — good (duplication remains until it's removed in S3a-2, but this is expected per deviation item #9).\n\nInteresting point: head_ws_test currently uses the shared net. However, the shared net's `unlisten!` also deposits `:stopped` (deviation item #8). In head_ws_test, the server (head.ws composition) reads the lifecycle medium — does head.ws's serve-step treat `:stopped` on the lifecycle as fatal? Let me check head.ws's lifecycle handling and the restart test. The engineer said \"the restart case still passes because that server simply becomes :stopped before being replaced.\" Let me quickly verify head.ws's serve-step handling.\n\nAlso check head_ws_test's real-socket JVM case — does it use unlisten!? If so, then ws/jvm.clj's `stop-listening!` etc.\n\nNext, check the documentation changes (dao.stream.remote.md, dao.stream.ws.md) — these have been modified. And verify the invariant \"no new core stream operations\" — remote_channel only uses stream/append!, next, cursor, close!, descriptor. No problems.\n\nFurther verification: remote-channel's `serve` — regarding the spec §2.1 requirement \"the server value includes `:stop` (`{:since now :outcome o}` after initiation), and on refusal `:reason` and details.\" In the refusal case, the :status key is... `refused` merges `{:status :refused :reason reason}` with data. In the bind-failed case during serve, data is {} — no details. In observe's bind-failed case, it's {:detail value}. No problem.\n\nAnother spec conformance check: §2.1 states \"serve validates bounds by passing them to make-endpoint, make-acceptor, and links.\" The engineer calls make-endpoint, make-acceptor, and links. ✓.\n\nBut an ordering issue: in `serve`, make-endpoint and make-acceptor run before `(link-policy b)`. If bounds are valid for endpoint/acceptor but the link policy is invalid, links will throw — before bind!. ✓ The test asserts that nothing is listening.\n\nWait — actually there's a subtle bug: `_ (link-policy b)` is called after make-acceptor. But make-acceptor could throw first for invalid bounds — that's still a composition error, so no problem.\n\nNext, potential real defects to consider:\n\nA. `serve-step` during `:starting`/`:serving` — drain-lifecycle first, then accept-step!. But the spec §4.3 order for the first stopping tick: accept-step! once, then close-sessions!+endpoint-stop!, then unbind!. In `begin-stop`, after accept-step! there's release! — but release! calls ws-project/stop! again (idempotent swap). No problem.\n\nB. `begin-stop`: if `unbind!` returns false because `:unbind!` doesn't exist on host ((fn? f) is false → returns false) → stopped ::unbind-failed. But a host without :unbind! is permitted (\"any of the three may be missing\")! §2.1 host ::= {:connect! f :bind! g :unbind! h}, any may be missing. If unbind! is absent, stop completes immediately as :stopped with outcome ::unbind-failed. Hmm — is this right? Spec §4.3: \"3. unbind! with the server's deposit!; throws or non-ok response records :stop {:outcome ::unbind-failed}, server is immediately :stopped.\" If unbind! is missing... nothing to release; arguably it should become :stopped unconfirmed, or immediately confirmed. Marking it as \"unbind-failed\" when the host simply doesn't have unbind! could be called a mislabel, but the consequence (immediately :stopped) is safe. Wait — but is that really so? If the host has no :unbind!, the listener stays alive (net still routes accept! to the endpoint). The server is :stopped but the listener is still bound and accept-connection! still accepts! Spec §4.1: \"subsequent accept-connection! continues to function unless the host listener is gone — that's the host's unbind!\". If unbind! is absent, subsequent connections would be accepted into pending slots and then... endpoint-stop! is only called during release! in the stopping tick; after :stopped, serve-step returns the server unchanged (case's default branch). So pending connections leak — accepted but never closed (unless the host itself times out, or the expiry inside endpoint-step... which never runs). Hmm. But wait: in that case, do offers reach the acceptor? accept-step! never runs after :stopped. Slots stay :pending until expiry-ms — but nothing steps the endpoint. Therefore, hosts lacking :unbind! leak live sockets after stop.\n\nHow realistic is this? yin.repl.host/websocket — do all three hosts provide unbind!? Let me check. JVM: dao.stream.ws.jvm's stop-listening!; Node: stop-listening!. yin.repl.host presumably wires :unbind! for JVM/Node. If unbind! always exists in real assemblies, the edge case is only for test hosts. Still, labeling \"no unbind! on host\" as ::unbind-failed and immediately marking :stopped is defensible (nothing more can be done), but leaking accepted pending connections is a defect worth pointing out. Actually wait — in begin-stop, release! ran endpoint-stop! before unbind!. After :stopped, a new accept-connection! (listener still alive) creates a new pending slot; nothing closes it. But this only happens when :unbind! is absent. Worth noting.\n\nActually let me re-check: `unbind!` returns `(and (fn? f) (try ...))` — f missing → false → stopped ::unbind-failed. The test `stop-while-starting-and-stop-twice-are-idempotent` uses a quiet bind host but keeps :unbind! from host-of. Hmm, `stop-completes-without-a-close-callback` overrides :unbind! with one that returns ok without depositing → unconfirmed after grace period. OK.\n\nC. `continue-stop` runs accept-step! every tick — which includes reap-sessions! and per-session mirror. Sessions were closed on the first tick and reaped at the end of the first tick? In begin-stop: accept-step! (reaps nothing yet — sessions aren't closed yet), release! closes sessions. Then the next tick's continue-stop: accept-step! → reaps the closed ones. The test asserts sessions are empty after the second tick. ✓\n\nD. Race between dial-step's :resolving and a server that has already refused? Irrelevant.\n\nE. `dial`'s events: `(when events {...})` — if the caller passes events as nil, the key is omitted. No problem.\n\nF. `close!` on dial during :resolving with a channel established but never attached: closes the ws handle. ✓ Also closes the traffic ring? No — traffic medium and ring remain open. Spec §4.4 doesn't require it. The dial's ring/traffic are fresh per dial and discarded. OK.\n\nG. `channel-lost?` on dial — after close!, the status is :closed and dial-step doesn't step. ✓\n\nH. endpoint-stop!: what about slots where `(:handle-state slot)` is nil? Only :pending slots have handle-state. ✓ Also `terminal!` emits :ws/closed via deposit! — deposit! appends to the control medium; if append fails (control full), deposit! closes... already closed. OK.\n\nI. `stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced` test — asserts `(is (every? :closed? (vals (rc/sessions @(:server w)))) \"sessions closed\")` after the first stopping tick. close-sessions! marks :closed?. ✓\n\nJ. Invariants: \"no ambient scheduler\" — none. \"No new core stream operations\" — endpoint-stop!/stop!/close-sessions! are composition operations on existing handles, not core stream protocol operations. ✓ Transport neutrality: remote-channel is under dao.stream and speaks only ws. ✓ The yin namespace is unchanged except for tests. Verify: git status shows only changes in dao/stream sources + test + docs + head_ws_test. src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc unchanged. ✓ (Status shows modified: docs, ws.cljc, ws_project.cljc, ws_project_test, ws_test, head_ws_test; new: remote_channel.cljc, loopback_net.cljc, remote_channel_test.cljc.)\n\nK. head_ws_test with the moved net: the net's `unlisten!` currently deposits `:stopped`. head.ws's serve-step — how does it handle a lifecycle `:stopped` fact? Let me check head.ws's lifecycle handling to see whether an unexpected :stopped deposition breaks a head.ws case (regression risk from deviation item #8). Also net's `close-conn!`: in the original head_ws fixture, did close-conn! call the server's closed! before the client's? If the order changed, could that break a head_ws test? Let me diff mentally — I can't diff, but I'll check head.ws's lifecycle handling.\n\nL. Another check: remote-channel's `serve` passes `:control (writer-target (buffer capacity))` and `:control-admission (admission capacity)` — the admission's value-domain is :portable-values. The control medium carries :ws/closed events with attachment id (string) and host handles? Offers carry host handles via slot offer media with handoff-admission :host-values. Control events: `{:ws/attachment id :ws/event ...}` — portable values. But wait, ws/accept-connection!'s pending handle emits events on the control medium with only portable keys. ✓ head.ws presumably did the same thing.\n\nM. `make-slots` — offer/ack buffers with capacity 1, cursors issued at anchor-newest. Slot admission: handoff-admission {:retention :evict-oldest :capacity 1 :value-domain :host-values}. ws_test's handoff-slot uses the same shape. ✓\n\nN. Check spec: server value keys — `:spec`, `:descriptor`, `:endpoint`, `:acceptor`, `:listener`, `:lifecycle`, `:lifecycle-cursor`, `:lifecycle-gaps`, `:stop`, `:reason`. The implementation also has `:host`, `:bounds`, `:deposit!`. Additional items are fine (tests use :deposit! for the lifecycle-gap case — needed to inject facts; the spec's own tests say \"push 70 :listener-error facts through deposit!,\" so exposing deposit! in the value is intended).\n\nO. Spec §2.1's dial value keys: `{:status :spec :descriptor :name :dial :handle :identity :outcome}` — implementation matches.\n\nP. dial-step spec: \":attached: when the channel's projection is closed (ws-project/closed? of (:project (ws-project/channel dial))) → :lost\" plus the engineer's :gone?. ✓\n\nQ. resolve-step: on ok → dial-reflect! of `(:dao.stream/descriptor r)`; spec says \"ok → dial-reflect! of the returned descriptor → :attached with :handle, :identity.\" ✓ Note: `:identity (:dao.stream/identity r)` — from the resolve outcome. ✓\n\nR. Hmm, in resolve-step if dial-reflect! fails → :lost with outcome a. Spec doesn't state explicitly; reasonable.\n\nS. Potential defect: in `dial-step`, if status is :resolving and the channel has vanished (server refused mid-resolve), resolve-step gets transport-error channel-gone without retry? → :lost. ✓\n\nT. `run-until` in tests with dt=0 — no problem.\n\nU. Test `a-lifecycle-gap-while-starting-is-terminal` — quiet's bind wraps deposit! to swallow :bind-succeeded. But `listen-on` has already registered the listener with the wrapped deposit! — unlisten etc. No problem. After refusal, asserts unbind! was called and slots were released. Note: refusal path — `lifecycle-lost` during :starting calls `(unbind! server)` — but wait, at that moment the server's `:listener` is set (bind succeeded). ✓ endpoint-stop! closes the pending dial connection. ✓\n\nV. Documentation: verify changes in dao.stream.remote.md §3.0/§3.1 and dao.stream.ws.md. Let me read the relevant sections.\n\nW. Also check whether ws.cljc's `closed!` function handles host close callbacks → terminal. And check `head.ws`'s serve-step lifecycle handling for :stopped (regression check for deviation item #8 on head_ws_test's restart case). The restart case: the server's port is unlistened, then a new serve on the same port. In head.ws, when the lifecycle receives `:stopped`... if head.ws treats an unexpected :stopped as fatal → the server becomes stopped; then the new serve replaces it. The engineer said it passes. But also the real-socket JVM case: does it use net's `unlisten!`? No — real sockets use ws/jvm's stop-listening!. No problem.\n\nLet me check head.ws's serve-step/lifecycle handling.\n\nThe fixture relocation touches `head_ws_test`, so let's check how `head.ws` handles the lifecycle `:stopped` fact (deviation #8), and also check the documentation changes."}}
