{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"`make-endpoint` validates the descriptor via `descriptor?`, which requires `canonical-path?` — so a spec without `:path` (nil) will throw from make-endpoint inside `serve` as a composition error. This isn't refusal-as-data. Spec §2.1 lists as refusals no-transport, no-port, bind-failed, lifecycle-lost, invalid-table — a malformed path isn't included in the refusal list, and \"composition errors from any of them are re-thrown as the composition errors they are, before anything is listened to.\" So throwing for a nil path is per spec. Hmm, but is that really the case? The spec validates `:port` as refusal data, but the descriptor gate handles the path. Consistent with the spec.\n\nBut wait — there's an asymmetry: `:port` is validated as refusal `::no-port` before make-endpoint, but the descriptor? inside make-endpoint also requires a pos-int port. No problem.\n\nAlso regarding `dial`: descriptor-of is computed, but does dial validate the descriptor? `ws-project/dial-resolve!` → establish! → attach! validates the descriptor → invalid-descriptor result → resolve-step... no wait: establish! returns r (attach's result), which is non-ok → dial-resolve! responds with that outcome → the cond in resolve-step: transport-error? invalid-descriptor isn't transport-error → :else → :lost with outcome invalid-descriptor. Hmm, actually `(or (when (nil? @channel) (establish! dial cd)) ((:resolve! @channel) ...))` — establish! returns non-nil r on failure, so dial-resolve! answers with it. Then dial-step: status :lost, outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}. Reasonable.\n\nNow, remaining checks:\n- `ask!` on a test whose ring stays open (a-lost-close-event...) — no problem.\n- remote-channel's `dial` composes `:cursor (mint traffic stream/anchor-newest)` before attach! — required per ws-project/dial's docs. ✓\n- In remote-channel/dial, `traffic` buffer capacity comes from bounds. The attacher's admission is `:portable-values` with the same capacity. head.ws probably did the same thing.\n\nNext, potential issues: **the test `a-lifecycle-gap-while-serving` asserts diagnostics count 8 and gaps 1.** Deposit 70 facts into a capacity-64 ring; the cursor is issued at anchor-newest at serve time. The server ticks once at 0 (bind-succeeded is read). Then 70 deposits → the ring holds the last 64; read from cursor → gap → recovery cursor → read remaining facts. drain-lifecycle: first next → gap → lifecycle-lost increments gaps and adopts the cursor; loop continues; reads up to 64 listener-error facts → diagnose keeps the last 8. ✓ But wait: the gap response's cursor — after adopting the recovery cursor, do the remaining facts include the evicted ones? No: the recovery cursor points to the oldest retained. 70 deposited, 64 retained (facts 6..69). Gap count 1. ✓ Also `tick!` at 1 runs serve-step → drain-lifecycle → then accept-step! (still serving). Then attach! at 2. ✓\n\n- `a-host-that-stops-under-us`: unlisten! closes conns (server-side closed! → deposits :ws/closed on each session's traffic; client's closed! → deposits on dial's traffic), then deposits :stopped on the lifecycle. serve-step 1: drain-lifecycle reads :stopped → observe: catchall `(= :stopped kind)` → release! + stopped ::host-stopped. Note: drain runs before accept-step!, and since the status left starting/serving, accept-step! is skipped. Sessions closed via release!. Test asserts session's :closed? and ring closure. ✓ Then dial-step → :lost. ✓ Note: release! also runs endpoint-stop!. No problem.\n\nBut subtle point: in observe, the :stopped catchall runs in any live status other than stopping... including :starting (deviation #5, documented). ✓\n\n- Next, `stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced`: after `stop!`, asserts `(stream/append! reader-ws :still-open)` → ok. Then serve-step 10 → begin-stop: accept-step! at now=10. Wait — the pending late connection: accept-step!'s first endpoint-step (release dead pendings — late conn is still alive), then adopt offers: the late offer is read from the slot's offer medium → adopt! rejects (stopping) → closes handle via stream/close! → WsHandle's close! → invoke-close! 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\" → net enqueues close-conn → (later on pump) both sides closed!. Also terminal? close! doesn't emit a terminal — the host's close callback (closed!) emits the terminal :ws/closed. In the net, close-conn! calls the server's adapter's closed! → ws adapter's closed! → handle's closed! → terminal! :ws/closed to control. That happens on pump. accept-step!'s second endpoint-step: slot is still :pending with phase :closed → release-slot!. ✓ Then per-session pass: reader session's mirror answers the queued next request (the :value appended to source before stop... wait, poll! happened before stop; the request frame was pumped to server; the session's projection+mirror at begin-stop's accept-step! answers it — the answer is written to reader-ws → enqueued to client). Then release!: close-sessions! closes reader's handle (1000 detached) and ring; endpoint-stop! — pending slot already released, nothing. unbind! → count 1, listener dissoc'd, :stopped deposit enqueued.\n\nThen test asserts slots all free ✓, sessions closed ✓. pump → client receives answer + close. dial-step 10 → projection reads :ws/payload answer, then :ws/closed → closes ring; link drains filed answers. poll! h c → :value ✓. dial :lost? dial-step: after ws-project/dial-step!, status is :attached, channel-lost? → projection closed → :lost ✓.\n\nThen serve-step 20: continue-stop → accept-step! reaps closed sessions → drain-lifecycle reads :stopped → confirmed ✓. Calls still 1 ✓.\n\nAll consistent.\n\nNow, adversarial findings to consider:\n\n**Finding 1 (minor/edge): host without `:unbind!` stops as `::unbind-failed`, leaving the listener bound and future pending connections unclosed.** After :stopped, serve-step is inert, so a host that keeps routing upgrades (because it couldn't be unbound) will accumulate pending connections that are never closed nor expired (endpoint-step never runs again). Spec §2.1 states that any of the three host functions may be absent. Options: treat absence of :unbind! as unconfirmed → stopped after grace, or continue endpoint-stop! while connections exist. Severity: low (real assemblies all provide unbind!; yin.repl.host documents :unbind!). Worth recording.\n\nActually wait — does it matter? If no unbind!, `unbind!` returns false → `stopped ::unbind-failed` — server reaches terminal immediately while listener is live. endpoint-stop! was run once in release!. New connections accepted after that stay :pending forever with a live socket. expiry-ms is set (15000) but endpoint-step never runs again. This is a resource leak at that edge. Also the outcome label is misleading. Flag as minor defect / non-blocking.\n\n**Finding 2 (minor): `endpoint-stop!` ignores failure of `invoke-close!`.** No `:ws/error :ws/close-failure` diagnostic like WsHandle's close!/close-ended! emits. If host's close! throws, failure is silent. Terminal event is still deposited, slot released — bookkeeping doesn't depend on callback (D3 ✓). Minor nit.\n\n**Finding 3: `stopped` helper on host-stopped produces `:stop` without `:since`.** Shape is `{:outcome ...}`. Spec says `:stop {:since now :outcome o}` \"once initiated\" — host-stopped isn't initiated, so acceptable; but consumers expecting :since may trip. Nit.\n\n**Finding 4: `observe`'s `:stopped` catchall runs during `:starting` too.** Deviation #5, documented, reasonable.\n\n**Finding 5: `serve`'s bind-failed path discards endpoint/acceptor without `endpoint-stop!`.** If `bind!` threw after partially registering (e.g., net's listen-on throws before registering on port conflict — no pending). Can pending connections exist at the moment bind! answers non-ok? Only if host started accepting during the bind! call and then reported failure. Extreme edge; spec doesn't require. Skip or nit.\n\n**Finding 6: `release!` on `:stopping` → host-stopped (via observe's :stopped during :stopping is handled by stopped :confirmed; via lifecycle-lost during :stopping → unconfirmed).** Wait: lifecycle-lost during :stopping just returns `(stopped server ::unconfirmed)` without release! — but release! already ran on the first stopping tick (begin-stop). Subsequent ticks of continue-stop keep endpoint-stop! running. OK.\n\n**Finding 7: dial's `:events` in remote-channel is passed as raw handle, but does remote's emit! expect a writer?** emit! does `(stream/append! w v)` — works with handle. And does head.ws pass the events ring similarly? Quick check — head/ws.cljc dial's events. Probably the same. No problem.\n\n**Finding 8: `valid-table?` is structural check only** (deviation #2). Spec said `::invalid-table` is \"if any table entry's cursor holds a host object, i.e., make-acceptor throws.\" Does make-acceptor validate table entry handles? make-acceptor only requires `(map? table)` — doesn't validate entries at all! So the spec's claim that \"make-acceptor throws\" was itself inaccurate. Engineer's structural check (`:handle` exists) is a reasonable substitute. But what about a table entry whose `:handle` is a host object (e.g., a Java object)? `some?` passes. Then mirror-step fails per session → isolated by accept-step!'s try/catch → session closed. Hmm, does mirror-step validate table entries? dao.stream.remote mirror requires table entries to be handles/cursors. Invalid entry may throw inside session step → isolated. Also, `:value-domain :portable-values` admission of traffic medium means host objects can't cross the wire anyway. Deviation acceptable; note that `valid-table?` doesn't check that `:handle` is a stream reader — mirror-step validation handles that. Non-blocking finding.\n\n**Finding 9: `::invalid-table` refusal response includes `:spec` but not descriptor — no problem.**\n\n**Finding 10: Test coverage gaps against spec §7's list.** Spec enumerates 15 remote_channel cases; engineer states 15 cases landed. Comparing names:\n1. serve-formats-the-descriptor-below-the-boundary ✓\n2. a-reader-resolves-and-attaches-over-the-stepped-composition ✓\n3. production-bounds-reach-every-layer ✓\n4. idle-healthy-board-versus-blackholed-request ✓\n5. unrelated-traffic-flood-cannot-defer-expiry ✓\n6. a-lost-close-event-is-recovered-by-the-deadline ✓\n7. stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced ✓\n8. stop-completes-without-a-close-callback ✓\n9. stop-while-starting-and-stop-twice-are-idempotent ✓\n10. a-lifecycle-gap-while-serving-is-counted-and-serving-continues ✓\n11. a-lifecycle-gap-while-starting-is-terminal ✓\n12. a-host-that-stops-under-us-is-stopped-host-stopped ✓\n13. session-cap-and-idle-expiry-hold-under-the-profile ✓\n14. a-newcomer-during-stop-is-rejected ✓\n15. no-transport-and-no-port-are-refusals-as-data ✓\n\nSpec §7 also required ws_test case ✓ and two ws_project_test cases ✓. Note: spec's §7 for S3a-1 doesn't require head_board_test etc. (that's S3a-2).\n\nOne spec test detail: \"production-bounds-reach-every-layer: ... link policy (via next exceeding max-outstanding with a counting-writer-like seam, or by reading dial's :policy)\" — engineer reads :policy. Explicitly permitted. ✓\n\nAnother detail: spec's stop test says of pending connections \"both see 1001\"? No — that's the ws_test case; the remote-channel stop test follows deviation #3 not asserting 1001. Spec §7 says \"the reader's session handle and the pending handle are closed (both deposit :ws/closed), the slot frees, unbind! is called once.\" Test asserts conn closure, :ws/closed on control medium, slot free, unbind once. ✓ Deviation #3 justified — spec's own ordering (accept-step! before endpoint-stop!) means pending offers get rejected on adopt (1000), not endpoint-stop (1001). Actually wait — is that so? In begin-stop, first accept-step!: the late offer was already deposited on slot's offer medium. adopt! rejects and closes handle with generic close → 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\". Hmm, but is this the desired wire behavior for a connection rejected during stop? ws.md may assign 1001 \"going away\" semantics for server shutdown. 1000 for \"detached\" is a re-attachable normal closure — actually fits: the reader should re-attach. No problem.\n\nBut actually, hold on: does adopt!'s rejection close the handle before endpoint-step observes it? Order inside accept-step!: endpoint-step (releases dead pendings; late conn isn't dead yet), adopt loop reads offers → adopt! closes handle (phase :closed synchronously; net enqueues close callback), second endpoint-step → first doseq releases slots where handle-state phase is :closed → released. ✓ Test asserts slot free after serve-step 10 without pump — release is via phase check, not callback. ✓ Good: bookkeeping doesn't depend on callback.\n\n**Finding 11: `dial-step` at `:resolving` calls resolve-step once per tick.** Spec: \"one resolve per tick\". ✓\n\n**Finding 12: `channel-lost?` is checked only when `:attached`.** During :resolving, resolve outcome handles loss. ✓\n\n**Finding 13: In ws_project/dial-step!, `:gone?` set + handle close.** `(swap! channel assoc :gone? true)` — channel is an atom holding a map; no problem. But note: this runs only `(when-some [handle (:handle ch)])` — if ch is nil, stepped is nil, no-op. ✓ Also `stepped` is computed by `((:step! ch) (:cd ch) now)` — the link's step responds with a map including channel-gone?. In remote.cljc line 1128: step responds with `{:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? ...}`. ✓ And channel-loss! at line 711 clears outstanding and sets channel-gone? true.\n\nHmm, one question: if `(:step! ch)` is nil (channel not established), `stepped` is nil — `(when (:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? stepped))` on nil → nil. ✓\n\n**Finding 14: Does the addition of `:gone?` change ws_project/dial-step! behavior for existing consumers (head.ws)?** head.ws's dial-step calls ws-project/dial-step! — now the handle is also closed when a link expires. Previously (S2c), did dial-step! already close the handle on channel-gone? Docstring says \"if a request exceeds deadline, the channel is lost, and the dial that owns the connection closes the ws handle\" — was this there before, or is it new? Engineer states the only addition is recording `:gone? true`; close was already there (\"channel-loss!... dial-step! closes the ws handle\" is in spec §3.1 S2c). So the addition is only the flag + docstring. Existing head.ws consumers: head.ws's dial-step checks projection's closed? — with :gone?, head.ws doesn't read it; behavior unchanged aside from flag recording. Zero breakage ✓.\n\n**Finding 15: CLJD compatibility.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — engineer notes :cljd comes first elsewhere (payload-size comment states cljd first because cljd's host-eval reads :clj branches). In remote_channel.cljc line 322: `(catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ ...)` — :cljd first ✓. Line 355 same ✓. `case` on `(:status server)` with keyword constants — no problem. `mapv`, `select-keys`, `get-in` fine. `(assoc (:stop server) :outcome outcome)` — single-key assoc on nil map... CLJD's trap was \"multi-key assoc on nil\". Single-key assoc on nil is fine in Clojure; in CLJD? Probably fine. Hmm, worth flagging as unverified on Dart since Dart lane hasn't been run.\n\nAlso `doseq` with `map-indexed vector` — no problem. `take-last` no problem.\n\nOne cljd concern: `(def ^:private max-diagnostics 8)` no problem. `contains?` on sets/maps no problem.\n\n**Finding 16: `serve-step`'s case uses `(:starting :serving)` — a case with a list constant.** In Clojure's `case`, `(:starting :serving)` is treated as multiple test constants — valid. In CLJS, case with list of keywords compiles fine. CLJD? Engineer notes \"case on vector constant\" trap — this is a list and used elsewhere? head.ws's serve-step probably similar pattern. Quick check... actually remote_channel's `case (:status server)` with branches `(:starting :serving)`, `:stopping`, default `server`. CLJS's `case` supports multiple constants in a list. Should be fine in CLJD too, but unverified (Dart lane not run). Flag as verification debt.\n\n**Finding 17: `refused` at `::bind-failed` in serve drops `:host`, `:bounds`, `:deposit!`, `:lifecycle`** — so a bind-failed refusal can't drain lifecycle facts (e.g., an async :bind-failed arrives later). No problem — terminal.\n\nBut wait: async bind-failed during :starting is handled in observe. Synchronous non-ok from bind! → refuse immediately. ✓ Spec: \"if bind! throws, responds non-ok, or deposits :bind-failed\" — deposited case is handled by observe (test a-lifecycle... no, is there a test for bind-failed fact? Spec §7 doesn't enumerate; no problem).\n\n**Finding 18: `stop!` on a server whose bind! synchronously deposited facts but never ticked** — no problem.\n\n**Finding 19: `drain-lifecycle`'s default branch** — `case` with no match → `server` (the trailing expression acts as default). If outcome is blocked/other, returns server and loop exits? No — `case` responds with server, then `recur`? Let me look: `(let [r (stream/next ...)] (case (:dao.stream/outcome r) :ok (recur ...) :gap (recur ...) :end (lifecycle-lost server true) server))` — default responds with server, loop ends. ✓ blocked → exits loop. ✓\n\n**Finding 20: Test `production-bounds-reach-every-layer` composes server and dial with production bounds** — serve with production bounds calls bind! (listen-on) → listener registered on port 9 of new net. Also asserts `(empty? (:listeners @lnet))` only for the invalid bounds net. ✓\n\n**Finding 21: net's `flood!` delivers directly to client adapter, bypassing conn's closed? check?** It checks `(not @(:closed? conn))`. Delivers junk :ws/value frames to client → adapter's message! → handle's receive! → deposits :ws/payload on traffic → projection appends to ring. drain-budget 4 limits per-tick link drain. Test asserts expiry at deadline tick despite flood. ✓ Matches spec §7.\n\nNote: flood frames use attachment... `(transit/encode {:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value {:junk i}})` — delivered to client adapter which deposits with its own attachment. Ring capacity 64 (default) — junk appends may evict real answers. That's the point: even a flood that evicts doesn't defer expiry. ✓\n\n**Finding 22: `a-lost-close-event-is-recovered-by-the-deadline` manually appends directly to traffic with attachment \"other\"** — projection ignores other attachments but cursor... wait, traffic is dial's medium with capacity 8; the real :ws/closed for the dial's attachment was evicted by 8 fake events. Projection step: reads events, ignores \"other\" attachment, no :ws/closed → ring stays open. Then link's deadline (give-up-after 150, request sent at 1000... ask! at 1000; ticks 1050, 1150 → at 1150 deadline 1150 ≤ 1150 → expired → channel-loss! → :gone? → dial :lost. Test asserts lost after tick 1160. ✓ And `ask! h c` responds with channel-gone reason. ✓ This verifies the :gone? path (projection not closed). Good — the engineer's ws_project addition is directly tested here (line 324 asserts projection is not closed).\n\n**Finding 23: Invariant \"no new core stream ops\"** — check that no new protocol methods added to dao/stream.cljc — git status shows dao/stream.cljc unchanged. ✓ Only ws.cljc, ws_project.cljc modified, remote_channel.cljc new. ✓\n\n**Finding 24: Spec §4.3 says for first stopping tick \"2. ws-project/close-sessions!, then ws/endpoint-stop!. 3. unbind!...throw or non-ok response records... server is immediately :stopped (no subsequent completion can arrive).\"** Implementation ✓. \"Subsequent ticks: drain lifecycle medium; :stopped fact → confirmed; else grace → unconfirmed.\" Implementation additionally runs accept-step! + endpoint-stop! (deviation #4) — justified: reaps closed sessions and closes any late pendings. Does this violate anything? accept-step! during stopping rejects offers; mirror pass on remaining (closed) sessions — sessions marked :closed? are reaped at the end of accept-step!; before reap, per-session pass runs on closed sessions: step! on closed projection no-ops; mirror-step on closed ring no-ops. No problem.\n\n**Finding 25: `begin-stop` — order of spec step 2 is close-sessions! then endpoint-stop!; release! does stop!, close-sessions!, endpoint-stop!. ✓ And step 1 accept-step! is before. ✓\n\n**Finding 26: Idempotency of endpoint-stop! with respect to `:connections` map**: release-slot! disconns. Test asserts connections empty. ✓\n\n**Finding 27: What about pending slots whose ack arrives between release! and endpoint-stop!?** Single-threaded; impossible.\n\n**Finding 28: `loopback-literal?` is public in remote-channel but unused in remote-channel itself** — spec §2.1 enumerates as part of the API (used in S3a-2's board gate). ✓ Clj-kondo might flag unused public? No, publics are fine.\n\n**Finding 29: head_ws_test still uses fixtures via `:refer [connect-on listen-on loopback-net pump! unlisten!]` — but original fixtures had `close-conn!`, `enqueue!` as private? Moved net makes close-conn! public (used in remote_channel_test). ✓\n\nDeviation #8 risk: net's `unlisten!` now deposits :stopped — head.ws's observe → :status :stopped. Are there head_ws_test cases that call unlisten! and expect the server to still be serving? Engineer says only restart case, and passes. Also dht head tests? They use their own world? test/yin/repl/dht_head_test — git status doesn't show changes. Do they use head_ws fixtures or their own? Not changed, so use whatever they used before — probably their own net or head_ws_test's? Only head_ws_test changed. OK.\n\n**Finding 30: Node/Dart lanes not run** — engineer acknowledges; spec §8 requires green bb test on all 3 lanes before commit. This is a landing condition, not necessarily a review rejection, but verdict should note it. Also kondo clean claim can't be independently verified here (shell blocked).\n\nLet me also double-check that remote_channel's `serve` binds with `{:endpoint endpoint ...}` — does host.cljc's bind! signature expect `:endpoint`? host.cljc line 28: `(fn [{:keys [endpoint bind-host bind-port path accept! deposit!]}] …)`. ✓ Compatible.\n\nAnd yin.repl.host/websocket's actual implementation — does the JVM's listen! take these keys? That's S3b's concern; seam matches documented shape.\n\nOne more potential defect: **`observe` on `:bind-succeeded` during `:serving`** (duplicate fact) → falls to :else → server unchanged. ✓ bind-succeeded during :stopping → unchanged (stops on :stopped or grace). ✓\n\n**`diagnose` during :starting** — listener-error before bind-succeeded is recorded, keeps :starting. ✓ Bounded to 8. ✓\n\nAlso check `serve-step`'s case default: `server` — for :refused/:stopped. ✓ Note: `case` with no match and default expression `server` as final — yes, lines 480-492: `(case (:status server) (:starting :serving) ... :stopping ... server)`. ✓\n\nAnother one: **`stop!` while `:starting` never observed bind-succeeded, and host's bind! already registered a listener that will deposit facts later** — handled.\n\n**Missing: remote-channel doesn't expose `:lifecycle-gaps` reset or diagnostics during stopping** — irrelevant.\n\nPotential issue in `continue-stop`: it calls `ws/endpoint-stop!` every tick even after unbind succeeded — closing any pending conns that raced in. Good.\n\nEdge: **grace uses `(get-in server [:bounds :stop-grace-ms])`** — if bounds override stop-grace-ms to nil, `(>= x nil)` throws. Bounds merge on production-bounds, so nil only if caller explicitly sets nil. make-* layers don't validate stop-grace-ms. Minor robustness nit — explicit nil override crashes serve-step during stopping. Tests use {:stop-grace-ms 100}. Not a real-world defect.\n\nEdge: **`begin-stop` when `:acceptor` is nil** — can't happen on :starting/:serving (refused servers aren't stopping). stop! on refused server is unchanged. ✓\n\nNow the test count claims: 15 tests in remote_channel_test — let me count deftests: serve-formats (1), a-reader (2), production-bounds (3), idle-healthy (4), flood (5), lost-close (6), stop-explicit (7), stop-no-callback (8), stop-while-starting (9), gap-serving (10), gap-starting (11), host-stopped (12), session-cap (13), newcomer (14), no-transport (15). ✓ 15 cases, matches spec list exactly.\n\nNow, let me quickly verify the ws.cljc diff doesn't change anything besides endpoint-stop!. Without git access, grep for suspicious changes... status shows ws.cljc modified — probably only endpoint-stop! + docstring additions. Trust the reading.\n\nAlso, should verify the test world `serve` helper passes bounds {} → merge with production. In `world`, dial uses same bounds including :capacity overrides in lost-close test (capacity 8). ✓ Note: capacity 8 also shrinks server's traffic/rings — no problem.\n\nOne more check — `settle` uses `tick!` with same `now` repeatedly, and `run-until w now 0 50` dt=0. Deadline uses now + give-up; with now 0, no expiry. No problem.\n\n`read-past-value` is attach! then with same now — ok.\n\n**session-cap test check**: 3 dials, max-sessions 2. run-until all dials non-resolving. Third dial: offer rejected at cap → handle closed with 1000 → client's closed! → traffic :ws/closed → projection closes ring → resolve responds with channel-gone transport-error non-retryable → :lost. Test asserts outcome is transport-error. ✓ (Spec says \"third dial resolves to transport-error\" ✓.)\n\nThen busy polls each 10ms until 200; idle-timeout 100 → idle session reaped at last-activity + 100. Busy session's last-activity updated when mirror cursor moves... busy polls at cursor cb — `poll! hb cb` responds with blocked (prefetched?) — hmm, does a blocked poll update last-activity? active? checks whether projection's read cursor or mirror cursor moved. Poll responds locally from prefetched blocked... wait, deviation #7: reading value prefetched a blocked for next cursor; first poll responds locally without a wire request. But subsequent polls? ask! polls twice to get request on wire. In session-cap test's loop, only single `poll!` per tick — after first local blocked consumed...? Actually poll! h cb: reflection next at cursor cb. If link has filed blocked for cb, responds locally and... does re-request? Mirror on server answers next requests. Single poll per tick — wire request may or may not be sent; but active? on server — session's mirror cursor moves when server mirror answers... if nothing on wire, server session idles!\n\nHmm, but test claims busy is retained. Why? Because each poll that doesn't find a filed answer sends a next request over the wire → server mirror answers → server session's mirror cursor advances → active? true → last-activity updated. Deviation #7 explains: first poll after value read is answered locally (filed blocked), second poll sends request. In loop, poll every tick: tick n sends request (or responds locally), server answers, tick n+1 poll consumes filed answer, etc. Alternating — at least every other tick the server sees activity, well under idle-timeout 100 with dt=10. ✓ Reasonable.\n\nIdle dial: no polls → no wire traffic → session idles → reaped after 100ms → its handle+ring closed → client gets :ws/closed → projection closes → dial-step detects closed → :lost. ✓ Test then asserts ask! hi ci → channel-gone. ✓\n\nAlright. Pretty thorough. Now also confirm `terminal!` in endpoint-stop! emits with correct attachment — `(:attachment slot)`. ✓\n\nAlso **check accept-connection! after endpoint-stop!** — spec §4.1: \"subsequent accept-connection! continues to work unless host's listener is gone\". endpoint-stop! doesn't set a stopped flag on endpoint; new connections are accepted into freed slots. Matches spec (endpoint has no notion of stop). ✓\n\nNow let me also confirm nothing else changed in ws_project.cljc besides stop!/close-sessions!/adopt!'s stopping branch/:gone?/docstrings. `sessions` fn exists (was it there before? spec references ws-project/sessions in remote-channel's sessions). Probably existed already... actually remote-channel calls ws-project/sessions; if new, no problem. Not critical.\n\nAlso confirm dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj etc. unchanged — git status confirms.\n\nDocs: §6 also required dao.stream.ws.md \"Deferred, Liveness item: rewrite\" — done (lines 775-784). ✓ ws.md Serving ownership item — done. ✓ remote.md §3.0 two items + §3.1 sentence — done. ✓\n\nVerdict: ACCEPT with minor findings and landing conditions (3-lane bb test + kondo at commit, per §8).\n\nWait — one more adversarial check on `serve`: spec §2.1 says the server value includes `:listener` (\"what :bind! responded with\"). Implementation is `:listener bound` ✓. And spec says refusal response includes `:reason` and details. ✓\n\nAlso spec says `sessions` responds with \"ws-project/sessions of acceptor, or nil\" ✓.\n\nAnd `descriptor-of` — spec says \":transport :ws is the sole value and default\". Implementation is `(= :ws (:transport spec :ws))` ✓. But descriptor-of ignores :transport key — if spec has :transport :udp, serve refuses before descriptor-of. dial also refuses. ✓\n\nAnother possible defect: in `serve`, `valid-table?` runs after port check, before endpoint composition — but spec's ordering of refusals: no-transport, no-port, bind-failed, lifecycle-lost, invalid-table. Order differs (invalid-table before bind) — no problem, all data.\n\nAlso `serve` composes slots and endpoint before it checks that bind! throws — wasted work but harmless.\n\n**`make-slots` uses `(mint offer stream/anchor-newest)`** — issued at newest so only future offers are read. ✓ Matches ws_test's handoff-slot.\n\n**Admission domain check:** offer medium carries a host handle (ws/accepted with handle) → :host-values ✓; ack carries deposit target (host handle) → :host-values ✓; make-endpoint validates exactly this. ✓\n\nAlso — `media-maker`'s admission `:portable-values` for traffic: traffic carries wire payloads (portable). ✓ Control's admission :portable-values; make-endpoint requires. ✓\n\nI think the review is complete. Also let me quickly scan head_ws_test.cljc changes for any fixture mismatch (e.g., does it still define its own enqueue/close-conn). Grep shows only 5 fixtures referenced. No problem.\n\nAlso verify engineer's claim of \"no :ws/ keys in yin src\" — that's S3a-2's invariant, not S3a-1.\n\nAlso double-check `ws.cljc` — any changes besides endpoint-stop!? Probably docstring \"endpoint stop\" mention. Without git diff, can't be certain there aren't sneaky changes. Let me quickly grep for other references to \"endpoint-stopped\" and look at the file listing in status. Risk acceptable; the file reads consistently.\n\nActually, let me try git diff via read-only means one more time — shell may have been blocked by permissions across the board (second error said --yolo required). So no.\n\nSince I can't run tests or diff, my verdict relies on code reading. I should state this constraint explicitly.\n\nNow write the report to ~/.commandcode/plans/ (plan-mode constraint) with a note that it should be copied to collab/1791388000000-reviewer-stream-s3a-1.qwen3.8-max.findings.md. Wait — the task says write to collab/...qwen3.8-max.findings.md but my agent identity is \"cmd\". Filename specifies qwen3.8-max (implementer model). There's also existing stdout log `1791388000000-reviewer-stream-s3a-1.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log`. In plan mode, can't write there anyway. Write full report to plans dir and instruct copy.\n\nActually, plan file should be a plan... but the deliverable here is the review report. Structure plan file as review report + a small \"landing steps\" section (copy to collab path, run 3-lane tests). That satisfies both.\n\nNow let me write the report.\n\nCompleted the full path of code reading: all three source files, the four test files, the moved fixtures, and the diffs of the two design documents. Verified all the key spec sections (§1, §2.1, §4, §5, §7) by cross-checking against the implementation line by line. Currently drafting the adversarial review report."}}
{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`make-endpoint` validates the descriptor via `descriptor?`, which requires `canonical-path?` — so a spec without `:path` (nil) will throw from make-endpoint inside `serve` as a composition error. This isn't refusal-as-data. Spec §2.1 lists as refusals no-transport, no-port, bind-failed, lifecycle-lost, invalid-table — a malformed path isn't included in the refusal list, and \"composition errors from any of them are re-thrown as the composition errors they are, before anything is listened to.\" So throwing for a nil path is per spec. Hmm, but is that really the case? The spec validates `:port` as refusal data, but the descriptor gate handles the path. Consistent with the spec.\n\nBut wait — there's an asymmetry: `:port` is validated as refusal `::no-port` before make-endpoint, but the descriptor? inside make-endpoint also requires a pos-int port. No problem.\n\nAlso regarding `dial`: descriptor-of is computed, but does dial validate the descriptor? `ws-project/dial-resolve!` → establish! → attach! validates the descriptor → invalid-descriptor result → resolve-step... no wait: establish! returns r (attach's result), which is non-ok → dial-resolve! responds with that outcome → the cond in resolve-step: transport-error? invalid-descriptor isn't transport-error → :else → :lost with outcome invalid-descriptor. Hmm, actually `(or (when (nil? @channel) (establish! dial cd)) ((:resolve! @channel) ...))` — establish! returns non-nil r on failure, so dial-resolve! answers with it. Then dial-step: status :lost, outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}. Reasonable.\n\nNow, remaining checks:\n- `ask!` on a test whose ring stays open (a-lost-close-event...) — no problem.\n- remote-channel's `dial` composes `:cursor (mint traffic stream/anchor-newest)` before attach! — required per ws-project/dial's docs. ✓\n- In remote-channel/dial, `traffic` buffer capacity comes from bounds. The attacher's admission is `:portable-values` with the same capacity. head.ws probably did the same thing.\n\nNext, potential issues: **the test `a-lifecycle-gap-while-serving` asserts diagnostics count 8 and gaps 1.** Deposit 70 facts into a capacity-64 ring; the cursor is issued at anchor-newest at serve time. The server ticks once at 0 (bind-succeeded is read). Then 70 deposits → the ring holds the last 64; read from cursor → gap → recovery cursor → read remaining facts. drain-lifecycle: first next → gap → lifecycle-lost increments gaps and adopts the cursor; loop continues; reads up to 64 listener-error facts → diagnose keeps the last 8. ✓ But wait: the gap response's cursor — after adopting the recovery cursor, do the remaining facts include the evicted ones? No: the recovery cursor points to the oldest retained. 70 deposited, 64 retained (facts 6..69). Gap count 1. ✓ Also `tick!` at 1 runs serve-step → drain-lifecycle → then accept-step! (still serving). Then attach! at 2. ✓\n\n- `a-host-that-stops-under-us`: unlisten! closes conns (server-side closed! → deposits :ws/closed on each session's traffic; client's closed! → deposits on dial's traffic), then deposits :stopped on the lifecycle. serve-step 1: drain-lifecycle reads :stopped → observe: catchall `(= :stopped kind)` → release! + stopped ::host-stopped. Note: drain runs before accept-step!, and since the status left starting/serving, accept-step! is skipped. Sessions closed via release!. Test asserts session's :closed? and ring closure. ✓ Then dial-step → :lost. ✓ Note: release! also runs endpoint-stop!. No problem.\n\nBut subtle point: in observe, the :stopped catchall runs in any live status other than stopping... including :starting (deviation #5, documented). ✓\n\n- Next, `stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced`: after `stop!`, asserts `(stream/append! reader-ws :still-open)` → ok. Then serve-step 10 → begin-stop: accept-step! at now=10. Wait — the pending late connection: accept-step!'s first endpoint-step (release dead pendings — late conn is still alive), then adopt offers: the late offer is read from the slot's offer medium → adopt! rejects (stopping) → closes handle via stream/close! → WsHandle's close! → invoke-close! 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\" → net enqueues close-conn → (later on pump) both sides closed!. Also terminal? close! doesn't emit a terminal — the host's close callback (closed!) emits the terminal :ws/closed. In the net, close-conn! calls the server's adapter's closed! → ws adapter's closed! → handle's closed! → terminal! :ws/closed to control. That happens on pump. accept-step!'s second endpoint-step: slot is still :pending with phase :closed → release-slot!. ✓ Then per-session pass: reader session's mirror answers the queued next request (the :value appended to source before stop... wait, poll! happened before stop; the request frame was pumped to server; the session's projection+mirror at begin-stop's accept-step! answers it — the answer is written to reader-ws → enqueued to client). Then release!: close-sessions! closes reader's handle (1000 detached) and ring; endpoint-stop! — pending slot already released, nothing. unbind! → count 1, listener dissoc'd, :stopped deposit enqueued.\n\nThen test asserts slots all free ✓, sessions closed ✓. pump → client receives answer + close. dial-step 10 → projection reads :ws/payload answer, then :ws/closed → closes ring; link drains filed answers. poll! h c → :value ✓. dial :lost? dial-step: after ws-project/dial-step!, status is :attached, channel-lost? → projection closed → :lost ✓.\n\nThen serve-step 20: continue-stop → accept-step! reaps closed sessions → drain-lifecycle reads :stopped → confirmed ✓. Calls still 1 ✓.\n\nAll consistent.\n\nNow, adversarial findings to consider:\n\n**Finding 1 (minor/edge): host without `:unbind!` stops as `::unbind-failed`, leaving the listener bound and future pending connections unclosed.** After :stopped, serve-step is inert, so a host that keeps routing upgrades (because it couldn't be unbound) will accumulate pending connections that are never closed nor expired (endpoint-step never runs again). Spec §2.1 states that any of the three host functions may be absent. Options: treat absence of :unbind! as unconfirmed → stopped after grace, or continue endpoint-stop! while connections exist. Severity: low (real assemblies all provide unbind!; yin.repl.host documents :unbind!). Worth recording.\n\nActually wait — does it matter? If no unbind!, `unbind!` returns false → `stopped ::unbind-failed` — server reaches terminal immediately while listener is live. endpoint-stop! was run once in release!. New connections accepted after that stay :pending forever with a live socket. expiry-ms is set (15000) but endpoint-step never runs again. This is a resource leak at that edge. Also the outcome label is misleading. Flag as minor defect / non-blocking.\n\n**Finding 2 (minor): `endpoint-stop!` ignores failure of `invoke-close!`.** No `:ws/error :ws/close-failure` diagnostic like WsHandle's close!/close-ended! emits. If host's close! throws, failure is silent. Terminal event is still deposited, slot released — bookkeeping doesn't depend on callback (D3 ✓). Minor nit.\n\n**Finding 3: `stopped` helper on host-stopped produces `:stop` without `:since`.** Shape is `{:outcome ...}`. Spec says `:stop {:since now :outcome o}` \"once initiated\" — host-stopped isn't initiated, so acceptable; but consumers expecting :since may trip. Nit.\n\n**Finding 4: `observe`'s `:stopped` catchall runs during `:starting` too.** Deviation #5, documented, reasonable.\n\n**Finding 5: `serve`'s bind-failed path discards endpoint/acceptor without `endpoint-stop!`.** If `bind!` threw after partially registering (e.g., net's listen-on throws before registering on port conflict — no pending). Can pending connections exist at the moment bind! answers non-ok? Only if host started accepting during the bind! call and then reported failure. Extreme edge; spec doesn't require. Skip or nit.\n\n**Finding 6: `release!` on `:stopping` → host-stopped (via observe's :stopped during :stopping is handled by stopped :confirmed; via lifecycle-lost during :stopping → unconfirmed).** Wait: lifecycle-lost during :stopping just returns `(stopped server ::unconfirmed)` without release! — but release! already ran on the first stopping tick (begin-stop). Subsequent ticks of continue-stop keep endpoint-stop! running. OK.\n\n**Finding 7: dial's `:events` in remote-channel is passed as raw handle, but does remote's emit! expect a writer?** emit! does `(stream/append! w v)` — works with handle. And does head.ws pass the events ring similarly? Quick check — head/ws.cljc dial's events. Probably the same. No problem.\n\n**Finding 8: `valid-table?` is structural check only** (deviation #2). Spec said `::invalid-table` is \"if any table entry's cursor holds a host object, i.e., make-acceptor throws.\" Does make-acceptor validate table entry handles? make-acceptor only requires `(map? table)` — doesn't validate entries at all! So the spec's claim that \"make-acceptor throws\" was itself inaccurate. Engineer's structural check (`:handle` exists) is a reasonable substitute. But what about a table entry whose `:handle` is a host object (e.g., a Java object)? `some?` passes. Then mirror-step fails per session → isolated by accept-step!'s try/catch → session closed. Hmm, does mirror-step validate table entries? dao.stream.remote mirror requires table entries to be handles/cursors. Invalid entry may throw inside session step → isolated. Also, `:value-domain :portable-values` admission of traffic medium means host objects can't cross the wire anyway. Deviation acceptable; note that `valid-table?` doesn't check that `:handle` is a stream reader — mirror-step validation handles that. Non-blocking finding.\n\n**Finding 9: `::invalid-table` refusal response includes `:spec` but not descriptor — no problem.**\n\n**Finding 10: Test coverage gaps against spec §7's list.** Spec enumerates 15 remote_channel cases; engineer states 15 cases landed. Comparing names:\n1. serve-formats-the-descriptor-below-the-boundary ✓\n2. a-reader-resolves-and-attaches-over-the-stepped-composition ✓\n3. production-bounds-reach-every-layer ✓\n4. idle-healthy-board-versus-blackholed-request ✓\n5. unrelated-traffic-flood-cannot-defer-expiry ✓\n6. a-lost-close-event-is-recovered-by-the-deadline ✓\n7. stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced ✓\n8. stop-completes-without-a-close-callback ✓\n9. stop-while-starting-and-stop-twice-are-idempotent ✓\n10. a-lifecycle-gap-while-serving-is-counted-and-serving-continues ✓\n11. a-lifecycle-gap-while-starting-is-terminal ✓\n12. a-host-that-stops-under-us-is-stopped-host-stopped ✓\n13. session-cap-and-idle-expiry-hold-under-the-profile ✓\n14. a-newcomer-during-stop-is-rejected ✓\n15. no-transport-and-no-port-are-refusals-as-data ✓\n\nSpec §7 also required ws_test case ✓ and two ws_project_test cases ✓. Note: spec's §7 for S3a-1 doesn't require head_board_test etc. (that's S3a-2).\n\nOne spec test detail: \"production-bounds-reach-every-layer: ... link policy (via next exceeding max-outstanding with a counting-writer-like seam, or by reading dial's :policy)\" — engineer reads :policy. Explicitly permitted. ✓\n\nAnother detail: spec's stop test says of pending connections \"both see 1001\"? No — that's the ws_test case; the remote-channel stop test follows deviation #3 not asserting 1001. Spec §7 says \"the reader's session handle and the pending handle are closed (both deposit :ws/closed), the slot frees, unbind! is called once.\" Test asserts conn closure, :ws/closed on control medium, slot free, unbind once. ✓ Deviation #3 justified — spec's own ordering (accept-step! before endpoint-stop!) means pending offers get rejected on adopt (1000), not endpoint-stop (1001). Actually wait — is that so? In begin-stop, first accept-step!: the late offer was already deposited on slot's offer medium. adopt! rejects and closes handle with generic close → 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\". Hmm, but is this the desired wire behavior for a connection rejected during stop? ws.md may assign 1001 \"going away\" semantics for server shutdown. 1000 for \"detached\" is a re-attachable normal closure — actually fits: the reader should re-attach. No problem.\n\nBut actually, hold on: does adopt!'s rejection close the handle before endpoint-step observes it? Order inside accept-step!: endpoint-step (releases dead pendings; late conn isn't dead yet), adopt loop reads offers → adopt! closes handle (phase :closed synchronously; net enqueues close callback), second endpoint-step → first doseq releases slots where handle-state phase is :closed → released. ✓ Test asserts slot free after serve-step 10 without pump — release is via phase check, not callback. ✓ Good: bookkeeping doesn't depend on callback.\n\n**Finding 11: `dial-step` at `:resolving` calls resolve-step once per tick.** Spec: \"one resolve per tick\". ✓\n\n**Finding 12: `channel-lost?` is checked only when `:attached`.** During :resolving, resolve outcome handles loss. ✓\n\n**Finding 13: In ws_project/dial-step!, `:gone?` set + handle close.** `(swap! channel assoc :gone? true)` — channel is an atom holding a map; no problem. But note: this runs only `(when-some [handle (:handle ch)])` — if ch is nil, stepped is nil, no-op. ✓ Also `stepped` is computed by `((:step! ch) (:cd ch) now)` — the link's step responds with a map including channel-gone?. In remote.cljc line 1128: step responds with `{:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? ...}`. ✓ And channel-loss! at line 711 clears outstanding and sets channel-gone? true.\n\nHmm, one question: if `(:step! ch)` is nil (channel not established), `stepped` is nil — `(when (:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? stepped))` on nil → nil. ✓\n\n**Finding 14: Does the addition of `:gone?` change ws_project/dial-step! behavior for existing consumers (head.ws)?** head.ws's dial-step calls ws-project/dial-step! — now the handle is also closed when a link expires. Previously (S2c), did dial-step! already close the handle on channel-gone? Docstring says \"if a request exceeds deadline, the channel is lost, and the dial that owns the connection closes the ws handle\" — was this there before, or is it new? Engineer states the only addition is recording `:gone? true`; close was already there (\"channel-loss!... dial-step! closes the ws handle\" is in spec §3.1 S2c). So the addition is only the flag + docstring. Existing head.ws consumers: head.ws's dial-step checks projection's closed? — with :gone?, head.ws doesn't read it; behavior unchanged aside from flag recording. Zero breakage ✓.\n\n**Finding 15: CLJD compatibility.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — engineer notes :cljd comes first elsewhere (payload-size comment states cljd first because cljd's host-eval reads :clj branches). In remote_channel.cljc line 322: `(catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ ...)` — :cljd first ✓. Line 355 same ✓. `case` on `(:status server)` with keyword constants — no problem. `mapv`, `select-keys`, `get-in` fine. `(assoc (:stop server) :outcome outcome)` — single-key assoc on nil map... CLJD's trap was \"multi-key assoc on nil\". Single-key assoc on nil is fine in Clojure; in CLJD? Probably fine. Hmm, worth flagging as unverified on Dart since Dart lane hasn't been run.\n\nAlso `doseq` with `map-indexed vector` — no problem. `take-last` no problem.\n\nOne cljd concern: `(def ^:private max-diagnostics 8)` no problem. `contains?` on sets/maps no problem.\n\n**Finding 16: `serve-step`'s case uses `(:starting :serving)` — a case with a list constant.** In Clojure's `case`, `(:starting :serving)` is treated as multiple test constants — valid. In CLJS, case with list of keywords compiles fine. CLJD? Engineer notes \"case on vector constant\" trap — this is a list and used elsewhere? head.ws's serve-step probably similar pattern. Quick check... actually remote_channel's `case (:status server)` with branches `(:starting :serving)`, `:stopping`, default `server`. CLJS's `case` supports multiple constants in a list. Should be fine in CLJD too, but unverified (Dart lane not run). Flag as verification debt.\n\n**Finding 17: `refused` at `::bind-failed` in serve drops `:host`, `:bounds`, `:deposit!`, `:lifecycle`** — so a bind-failed refusal can't drain lifecycle facts (e.g., an async :bind-failed arrives later). No problem — terminal.\n\nBut wait: async bind-failed during :starting is handled in observe. Synchronous non-ok from bind! → refuse immediately. ✓ Spec: \"if bind! throws, responds non-ok, or deposits :bind-failed\" — deposited case is handled by observe (test a-lifecycle... no, is there a test for bind-failed fact? Spec §7 doesn't enumerate; no problem).\n\n**Finding 18: `stop!` on a server whose bind! synchronously deposited facts but never ticked** — no problem.\n\n**Finding 19: `drain-lifecycle`'s default branch** — `case` with no match → `server` (the trailing expression acts as default). If outcome is blocked/other, returns server and loop exits? No — `case` responds with server, then `recur`? Let me look: `(let [r (stream/next ...)] (case (:dao.stream/outcome r) :ok (recur ...) :gap (recur ...) :end (lifecycle-lost server true) server))` — default responds with server, loop ends. ✓ blocked → exits loop. ✓\n\n**Finding 20: Test `production-bounds-reach-every-layer` composes server and dial with production bounds** — serve with production bounds calls bind! (listen-on) → listener registered on port 9 of new net. Also asserts `(empty? (:listeners @lnet))` only for the invalid bounds net. ✓\n\n**Finding 21: net's `flood!` delivers directly to client adapter, bypassing conn's closed? check?** It checks `(not @(:closed? conn))`. Delivers junk :ws/value frames to client → adapter's message! → handle's receive! → deposits :ws/payload on traffic → projection appends to ring. drain-budget 4 limits per-tick link drain. Test asserts expiry at deadline tick despite flood. ✓ Matches spec §7.\n\nNote: flood frames use attachment... `(transit/encode {:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value {:junk i}})` — delivered to client adapter which deposits with its own attachment. Ring capacity 64 (default) — junk appends may evict real answers. That's the point: even a flood that evicts doesn't defer expiry. ✓\n\n**Finding 22: `a-lost-close-event-is-recovered-by-the-deadline` manually appends directly to traffic with attachment \"other\"** — projection ignores other attachments but cursor... wait, traffic is dial's medium with capacity 8; the real :ws/closed for the dial's attachment was evicted by 8 fake events. Projection step: reads events, ignores \"other\" attachment, no :ws/closed → ring stays open. Then link's deadline (give-up-after 150, request sent at 1000... ask! at 1000; ticks 1050, 1150 → at 1150 deadline 1150 ≤ 1150 → expired → channel-loss! → :gone? → dial :lost. Test asserts lost after tick 1160. ✓ And `ask! h c` responds with channel-gone reason. ✓ This verifies the :gone? path (projection not closed). Good — the engineer's ws_project addition is directly tested here (line 324 asserts projection is not closed).\n\n**Finding 23: Invariant \"no new core stream ops\"** — check that no new protocol methods added to dao/stream.cljc — git status shows dao/stream.cljc unchanged. ✓ Only ws.cljc, ws_project.cljc modified, remote_channel.cljc new. ✓\n\n**Finding 24: Spec §4.3 says for first stopping tick \"2. ws-project/close-sessions!, then ws/endpoint-stop!. 3. unbind!...throw or non-ok response records... server is immediately :stopped (no subsequent completion can arrive).\"** Implementation ✓. \"Subsequent ticks: drain lifecycle medium; :stopped fact → confirmed; else grace → unconfirmed.\" Implementation additionally runs accept-step! + endpoint-stop! (deviation #4) — justified: reaps closed sessions and closes any late pendings. Does this violate anything? accept-step! during stopping rejects offers; mirror pass on remaining (closed) sessions — sessions marked :closed? are reaped at the end of accept-step!; before reap, per-session pass runs on closed sessions: step! on closed projection no-ops; mirror-step on closed ring no-ops. No problem.\n\n**Finding 25: `begin-stop` — order of spec step 2 is close-sessions! then endpoint-stop!; release! does stop!, close-sessions!, endpoint-stop!. ✓ And step 1 accept-step! is before. ✓\n\n**Finding 26: Idempotency of endpoint-stop! with respect to `:connections` map**: release-slot! disconns. Test asserts connections empty. ✓\n\n**Finding 27: What about pending slots whose ack arrives between release! and endpoint-stop!?** Single-threaded; impossible.\n\n**Finding 28: `loopback-literal?` is public in remote-channel but unused in remote-channel itself** — spec §2.1 enumerates as part of the API (used in S3a-2's board gate). ✓ Clj-kondo might flag unused public? No, publics are fine.\n\n**Finding 29: head_ws_test still uses fixtures via `:refer [connect-on listen-on loopback-net pump! unlisten!]` — but original fixtures had `close-conn!`, `enqueue!` as private? Moved net makes close-conn! public (used in remote_channel_test). ✓\n\nDeviation #8 risk: net's `unlisten!` now deposits :stopped — head.ws's observe → :status :stopped. Are there head_ws_test cases that call unlisten! and expect the server to still be serving? Engineer says only restart case, and passes. Also dht head tests? They use their own world? test/yin/repl/dht_head_test — git status doesn't show changes. Do they use head_ws fixtures or their own? Not changed, so use whatever they used before — probably their own net or head_ws_test's? Only head_ws_test changed. OK.\n\n**Finding 30: Node/Dart lanes not run** — engineer acknowledges; spec §8 requires green bb test on all 3 lanes before commit. This is a landing condition, not necessarily a review rejection, but verdict should note it. Also kondo clean claim can't be independently verified here (shell blocked).\n\nLet me also double-check that remote_channel's `serve` binds with `{:endpoint endpoint ...}` — does host.cljc's bind! signature expect `:endpoint`? host.cljc line 28: `(fn [{:keys [endpoint bind-host bind-port path accept! deposit!]}] …)`. ✓ Compatible.\n\nAnd yin.repl.host/websocket's actual implementation — does the JVM's listen! take these keys? That's S3b's concern; seam matches documented shape.\n\nOne more potential defect: **`observe` on `:bind-succeeded` during `:serving`** (duplicate fact) → falls to :else → server unchanged. ✓ bind-succeeded during :stopping → unchanged (stops on :stopped or grace). ✓\n\n**`diagnose` during :starting** — listener-error before bind-succeeded is recorded, keeps :starting. ✓ Bounded to 8. ✓\n\nAlso check `serve-step`'s case default: `server` — for :refused/:stopped. ✓ Note: `case` with no match and default expression `server` as final — yes, lines 480-492: `(case (:status server) (:starting :serving) ... :stopping ... server)`. ✓\n\nAnother one: **`stop!` while `:starting` never observed bind-succeeded, and host's bind! already registered a listener that will deposit facts later** — handled.\n\n**Missing: remote-channel doesn't expose `:lifecycle-gaps` reset or diagnostics during stopping** — irrelevant.\n\nPotential issue in `continue-stop`: it calls `ws/endpoint-stop!` every tick even after unbind succeeded — closing any pending conns that raced in. Good.\n\nEdge: **grace uses `(get-in server [:bounds :stop-grace-ms])`** — if bounds override stop-grace-ms to nil, `(>= x nil)` throws. Bounds merge on production-bounds, so nil only if caller explicitly sets nil. make-* layers don't validate stop-grace-ms. Minor robustness nit — explicit nil override crashes serve-step during stopping. Tests use {:stop-grace-ms 100}. Not a real-world defect.\n\nEdge: **`begin-stop` when `:acceptor` is nil** — can't happen on :starting/:serving (refused servers aren't stopping). stop! on refused server is unchanged. ✓\n\nNow the test count claims: 15 tests in remote_channel_test — let me count deftests: serve-formats (1), a-reader (2), production-bounds (3), idle-healthy (4), flood (5), lost-close (6), stop-explicit (7), stop-no-callback (8), stop-while-starting (9), gap-serving (10), gap-starting (11), host-stopped (12), session-cap (13), newcomer (14), no-transport (15). ✓ 15 cases, matches spec list exactly.\n\nNow, let me quickly verify the ws.cljc diff doesn't change anything besides endpoint-stop!. Without git access, grep for suspicious changes... status shows ws.cljc modified — probably only endpoint-stop! + docstring additions. Trust the reading.\n\nAlso, should verify the test world `serve` helper passes bounds {} → merge with production. In `world`, dial uses same bounds including :capacity overrides in lost-close test (capacity 8). ✓ Note: capacity 8 also shrinks server's traffic/rings — no problem.\n\nOne more check — `settle` uses `tick!` with same `now` repeatedly, and `run-until w now 0 50` dt=0. Deadline uses now + give-up; with now 0, no expiry. No problem.\n\n`read-past-value` is attach! then with same now — ok.\n\n**session-cap test check**: 3 dials, max-sessions 2. run-until all dials non-resolving. Third dial: offer rejected at cap → handle closed with 1000 → client's closed! → traffic :ws/closed → projection closes ring → resolve responds with channel-gone transport-error non-retryable → :lost. Test asserts outcome is transport-error. ✓ (Spec says \"third dial resolves to transport-error\" ✓.)\n\nThen busy polls each 10ms until 200; idle-timeout 100 → idle session reaped at last-activity + 100. Busy session's last-activity updated when mirror cursor moves... busy polls at cursor cb — `poll! hb cb` responds with blocked (prefetched?) — hmm, does a blocked poll update last-activity? active? checks whether projection's read cursor or mirror cursor moved. Poll responds locally from prefetched blocked... wait, deviation #7: reading value prefetched a blocked for next cursor; first poll responds locally without a wire request. But subsequent polls? ask! polls twice to get request on wire. In session-cap test's loop, only single `poll!` per tick — after first local blocked consumed...? Actually poll! h cb: reflection next at cursor cb. If link has filed blocked for cb, responds locally and... does re-request? Mirror on server answers next requests. Single poll per tick — wire request may or may not be sent; but active? on server — session's mirror cursor moves when server mirror answers... if nothing on wire, server session idles!\n\nHmm, but test claims busy is retained. Why? Because each poll that doesn't find a filed answer sends a next request over the wire → server mirror answers → server session's mirror cursor advances → active? true → last-activity updated. Deviation #7 explains: first poll after value read is answered locally (filed blocked), second poll sends request. In loop, poll every tick: tick n sends request (or responds locally), server answers, tick n+1 poll consumes filed answer, etc. Alternating — at least every other tick the server sees activity, well under idle-timeout 100 with dt=10. ✓ Reasonable.\n\nIdle dial: no polls → no wire traffic → session idles → reaped after 100ms → its handle+ring closed → client gets :ws/closed → projection closes → dial-step detects closed → :lost. ✓ Test then asserts ask! hi ci → channel-gone. ✓\n\nAlright. Pretty thorough. Now also confirm `terminal!` in endpoint-stop! emits with correct attachment — `(:attachment slot)`. ✓\n\nAlso **check accept-connection! after endpoint-stop!** — spec §4.1: \"subsequent accept-connection! continues to work unless host's listener is gone\". endpoint-stop! doesn't set a stopped flag on endpoint; new connections are accepted into freed slots. Matches spec (endpoint has no notion of stop). ✓\n\nNow let me also confirm nothing else changed in ws_project.cljc besides stop!/close-sessions!/adopt!'s stopping branch/:gone?/docstrings. `sessions` fn exists (was it there before? spec references ws-project/sessions in remote-channel's sessions). Probably existed already... actually remote-channel calls ws-project/sessions; if new, no problem. Not critical.\n\nAlso confirm dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj etc. unchanged — git status confirms.\n\nDocs: §6 also required dao.stream.ws.md \"Deferred, Liveness item: rewrite\" — done (lines 775-784). ✓ ws.md Serving ownership item — done. ✓ remote.md §3.0 two items + §3.1 sentence — done. ✓\n\nVerdict: ACCEPT with minor findings and landing conditions (3-lane bb test + kondo at commit, per §8).\n\nWait — one more adversarial check on `serve`: spec §2.1 says the server value includes `:listener` (\"what :bind! responded with\"). Implementation is `:listener bound` ✓. And spec says refusal response includes `:reason` and details. ✓\n\nAlso spec says `sessions` responds with \"ws-project/sessions of acceptor, or nil\" ✓.\n\nAnd `descriptor-of` — spec says \":transport :ws is the sole value and default\". Implementation is `(= :ws (:transport spec :ws))` ✓. But descriptor-of ignores :transport key — if spec has :transport :udp, serve refuses before descriptor-of. dial also refuses. ✓\n\nAnother possible defect: in `serve`, `valid-table?` runs after port check, before endpoint composition — but spec's ordering of refusals: no-transport, no-port, bind-failed, lifecycle-lost, invalid-table. Order differs (invalid-table before bind) — no problem, all data.\n\nAlso `serve` composes slots and endpoint before it checks that bind! throws — wasted work but harmless.\n\n**`make-slots` uses `(mint offer stream/anchor-newest)`** — issued at newest so only future offers are read. ✓ Matches ws_test's handoff-slot.\n\n**Admission domain check:** offer medium carries a host handle (ws/accepted with handle) → :host-values ✓; ack carries deposit target (host handle) → :host-values ✓; make-endpoint validates exactly this. ✓\n\nAlso — `media-maker`'s admission `:portable-values` for traffic: traffic carries wire payloads (portable). ✓ Control's admission :portable-values; make-endpoint requires. ✓\n\nI think the review is complete. Also let me quickly scan head_ws_test.cljc changes for any fixture mismatch (e.g., does it still define its own enqueue/close-conn). Grep shows only 5 fixtures referenced. No problem.\n\nAlso verify engineer's claim of \"no :ws/ keys in yin src\" — that's S3a-2's invariant, not S3a-1.\n\nAlso double-check `ws.cljc` — any changes besides endpoint-stop!? Probably docstring \"endpoint stop\" mention. Without git diff, can't be certain there aren't sneaky changes. Let me quickly grep for other references to \"endpoint-stopped\" and look at the file listing in status. Risk acceptable; the file reads consistently.\n\nActually, let me try git diff via read-only means one more time — shell may have been blocked by permissions across the board (second error said --yolo required). So no.\n\nSince I can't run tests or diff, my verdict relies on code reading. I should state this constraint explicitly.\n\nNow write the report to ~/.commandcode/plans/ (plan-mode constraint) with a note that it should be copied to collab/1791388000000-reviewer-stream-s3a-1.qwen3.8-max.findings.md. Wait — the task says write to collab/...qwen3.8-max.findings.md but my agent identity is \"cmd\". Filename specifies qwen3.8-max (implementer model). There's also existing stdout log `1791388000000-reviewer-stream-s3a-1.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log`. In plan mode, can't write there anyway. Write full report to plans dir and instruct copy.\n\nActually, plan file should be a plan... but the deliverable here is the review report. Structure plan file as review report + a small \"landing steps\" section (copy to collab path, run 3-lane tests). That satisfies both.\n\nNow let me write the report.\n\nCompleted the full path of code reading: all three source files, the four test files, the moved fixtures, and the diffs of the two design documents. Verified all the key spec sections (§1, §2.1, §4, §5, §7) by cross-checking against the implementation line by line. Currently drafting the adversarial review report.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_75080f13c73d4ec9ac1bc2a1","name":"write_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/.commandcode/plans/reviewer-stream-s3a-1-report.md","content":"# Reviewer report: Slice S3a-1 (stream-crossmachine-s3a)\n\n> Plan-mode note: this file is the finished review report. On exiting plan mode,\n> copy it verbatim to\n> `collab/1791388000000-reviewer-stream-s3a-1.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`\n> (the only write owed), then run the landing verification listed at the end.\n\n---\n\nCompleted-GMT: (fill at write time)\nCoding-Agent: cmd (reviewer, plan-mode read-only pass)\n\n# Reviewer: adversarial review of Track B Slice S3a-1\n\nReviewed at `stream-crossmachine-s3a` working tree (base `01138591`, uncommitted):\n`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc` (`endpoint-stop!`), `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc`\n(`stop!`, `close-sessions!`, `adopt!` stopping branch, `dial-step!` `:gone?`),\n`src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_channel.cljc` (new, whole), `test/dao/stream/loopback_net.cljc`\n(new), `test/dao/stream/remote_channel_test.cljc` (new, 15 cases), the additions to\n`test/dao/stream/ws_test.cljc` and `test/dao/stream/ws_project_test.cljc`, the fixture move\nin `test/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc`, and the `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md`\n§3.0/§3.1 and `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md` Serving/Deferred amendments, against the\narchitect spec (`collab/1791384000000-...findings.md`) and the engineer report\n(`collab/1791386000000-...findings.md`).\n\nMethod note: this pass is read-only; `git diff` and test runs were unavailable in the\nreview environment, so the engineer's green-run claims (3699 JVM tests, kondo clean) are\ntaken on report and re-verified at landing (see Conditions).\n\n## Verdict: ACCEPT\n\nThe implementation conforms to the S3a-1 spec on every blocking point I could check by\nreading. All nine engineer deviations are sound; two are places where the engineer is\nmore correct than the spec text. Findings below are minor and none blocks the commit;\nF1 and the landing conditions should be recorded for S3a-2/S4.\n\n## 1. Spec conformance, point by point\n\n- **§4.1 `endpoint-stop!`** (ws.cljc:762-780): per pending slot — phase `:closed`,\n  `invoke-close!` 1001 `\"dao.stream/endpoint-stopped\"`, `terminal!` `:ws/closed` on the\n  control medium, `release-slot!`. Idempotent (a second call finds no `:pending` slot);\n  answers the endpoint. `terminal!`'s `:terminal?` volatile guard keeps the terminal event\n  exactly-once even if a synchronous host close callback races it. Matches the ws.md\n  Serving ownership bullet as amended.\n- **§4.2 `stop!` / `close-sessions!`** (ws_project.cljc:428-448): `stop!` is a pure\n  `swap!` (no I/O); `adopt!`'s guard `(not (:stopping? @acceptor))` (line 331) rejects\n  offers exactly the way the cap does — handle closed, no ack, no media. Slot release does\n  not depend on the close callback: the second `endpoint-step` inside `accept-step!`\n  releases on the handle's `:closed` phase. `close-sessions!` closes resources outside\n  `swap!` and marks `:closed?`; the next `accept-step!` reaps — same discipline as\n  `reap-sessions!`.\n- **§4.3 stop order** (`begin-stop`, remote_channel.cljc:433-443): `accept-step!` (the\n  last bounded answering pass) → `release!` (`stop!` + `close-sessions!` +\n  `endpoint-stop!`) → `unbind!`, with `::unbind-failed` stopping at once. Later ticks\n  (`continue-stop`) complete on the host `:stopped` fact (`:confirmed`), a lifecycle\n  gap/end (`::unconfirmed`), or `stop-grace-ms` against driver `now` (`::unconfirmed`).\n  No sleep, no clock read. `stop!` on `:starting`/`:serving` only; refused/stopping/\n  stopped servers answered unchanged; `stop!` performs no I/O (test asserts `calls` 0).\n- **§4.4 `close!` on a dial** (remote_channel.cljc:612-624): closes the ws handle when a\n  channel exists, closes the reflection when attached (guarded by `stream/closable?`),\n  idempotent on `:closed`.\n- **§1 bounds profile**: all 20 table rows present with the exact spec values; the\n  docstring carries the sizing cross-checks. Validation is delegated: `make-endpoint`\n  (five `:ws/*` bounds + descriptor), `make-acceptor` (five step/session bounds),\n  `remote/links` (four link bounds) all run before `bind!` — the test asserts nothing\n  listened on invalid bounds.\n- **§2.1 API shape**: `serve`/`serve-step`/`stop!`/`sessions` and\n  `dial`/`dial-step`/`handle`/`close!`, `descriptor-of`, `loopback-literal?`,\n  `production-bounds` — all present with the spec'd server/dial value keys and the five\n  refusal reasons as data. `bind!`/`unbind!` are called with exactly the documented\n  `yin.repl.host` seam shapes (host.cljc:28-32), so S3b plugs in without adaptation.\n  `loopback-literal?` is a verbatim copy of `head.ws/loopback?` (duplication is\n  intentional and dies with the namespace in S3a-2).\n- **§5.1 lifecycle table**: `observe` + `lifecycle-lost` implement every cell — starting:\n  `bind-succeeded`→serving, `bind-failed`→refused with `:detail`, gap/end→`::lifecycle-lost`\n  with best-effort `unbind!` + `endpoint-stop!`; serving: diagnostics bounded to 8, gap\n  counted with cursor adopted, `:stopped`/end→`::host-stopped` after `release!`; stopping:\n  `:stopped`→`:confirmed`, gap/end→`::unconfirmed`. `drain-lifecycle` stops reading once\n  the status leaves `#{:starting :serving :stopping}`, and `serve-step` skips\n  `accept-step!` when draining refused the server mid-tick.\n- **§5.2 channel-drop rows**: the deadline-recovery row (close event evicted by a traffic\n  gap) is implemented by the `:gone?` flag and tested directly\n  (`a-lost-close-event-is-recovered-by-the-deadline` asserts the projection stays open and\n  the deadline still lands). No row invents a gap on the board; nothing touches yin.\n- **§7 test plan**: all 15 remote-channel cases landed with the spec'd names, plus the\n  `ws_test` endpoint-stop case (1001, both terminal events, slots free, late ack stale,\n  second stop a no-op) and the two `ws_project_test` cases (stopping reject + last\n  answering pass; close-sessions + reap). The loopback net moved with `blackhole!` and\n  `flood!` as pure additions; `head_ws_test` now consumes the shared fixture.\n- **Invariants**: no ambient clock or scheduler anywhere in the new code (every deadline\n  is driver `now`); no new core stream operation (`dao/stream.cljc` untouched — only\n  `append!`/`next`/`cursor`/`close!`/`descriptor` composed); no public `closed?` on\n  handles; transport neutrality held — `remote_channel.cljc` lives below the boundary,\n  yin sources are untouched, and the portable spec carries no `:ws/` key (asserted).\n\n## 2. Engineer deviations 1-9: assessment\n\n1. **20 profile keys, not 17** — correct; the §1 table has 20 rows and the brief's \"17\"\n   counted only the layer-enforced bounds. The test pins the count at 20. Accepted.\n2. **Structural `::invalid-table` check** — correct and *more* right than the spec:\n   `make-acceptor` only checks `(map? table)` and never throws on entry cursors, so the\n   spec's \"i.e. `make-acceptor` throws\" was unimplementable as written, and catching its\n   throw would conflate invalid bounds (must rethrow) with invalid table (must refuse).\n   Note the check accepts any non-nil `:handle`; a non-stream handle fails later inside\n   the per-session isolation of `accept-step!`, which is bounded and acceptable. Accepted.\n3. **Pending-at-stop closed by `adopt!` (1000 detached), not `endpoint-stop!` (1001)** —\n   a direct consequence of the spec's own §4.3 order (`accept-step!` runs first and\n   consumes the offer). 1000 \"detached\" is the reattachable close the ws design assigns,\n   which is the right wire semantic for a board reader. `endpoint-stop!`'s 1001 path is\n   covered by the `ws_test` case and the lifecycle-lost-starting case. Accepted.\n4. **Later stopping ticks also run `accept-step!` + `endpoint-stop!`** — necessary, not\n   gratuitous: without it, `:closed?` sessions are never reaped (the test asserts\n   `sessions` empty after the confirming tick) and pendings that race in after the first\n   pass leak until the host closes them. Accepted.\n5. **`host-stopped` also runs `ws-project/stop!`; `:stopped` while starting is\n   host-stopped** — both close real holes (adoption after host-stopped; the unspecified\n   §5.1 starting/`:stopped` cell). Accepted.\n6. **Newcomer-during-stop outcome is transport-error/channel-gone, not the spec's\n   `:ws/transport-error` event text** — over the net the client socket exists before the\n   reject, so `:ws/closed` is what deposits; the observable dial status (`:lost`,\n   transport-error, no session) matches the spec's intent. Accepted.\n7. **Double poll (`ask!`) in liveness tests** — correct: reading the value files a local\n   `blocked` for the kept cursor, so a single poll would never put a request on the wire\n   and the test would prove nothing. Accepted.\n8. **`unlisten!` deposits `:stopped`** — required by the host-stopped case; `head.ws`'s\n   `observe` already maps `:stopped`→`:status :stopped`, so the head_ws restart case is\n   unaffected (the old server is replaced). The real-socket JVM case does not go through\n   the net. Accepted.\n9. **`yin.vm.linker.head.ws` left untouched** — matches the §8 landing table: S3a-1's\n   code column lists no yin file. Accepted.\n\nThe undocumented addition — `:gone?` on the dial channel (ws_project.cljc:650-653,\n`channel` docstring updated) — is a real gap-filler: when `:ws/closed` is evicted the\nprojection never closes, and without the flag a remote-channel dial would stay\n`:attached` forever after link expiry. It is additive (existing consumers do not read\nthe key), driver-paced, and directly tested. Accepted.\n\n## 3. Findings (non-blocking)\n\n- **F1 (minor defect, edge): a host without `:unbind!` stops as `::unbind-failed` with a\n  live listener.** `unbind!` (remote_channel.cljc:347-356) answers false when `:unbind!`\n  is absent, and `begin-stop` marks the server `:stopped` at once. But §2.1 says any of\n  the three host functions may be absent; with no `:unbind!`, nothing releases the\n  listener, and after `:stopped` `serve-step` is inert — so connections the still-bound\n  host accepts later sit `:pending` forever (`endpoint-step`'s expiry never runs again).\n  Real assemblies (`yin.repl.host`) always provide `:unbind!`, so impact is test-hosts\n  only. Suggested fix in S3b: when `:unbind!` is absent, stop as `::unconfirmed` via the\n  grace path instead of `::unbind-failed`, or keep running `endpoint-stop!` while any\n  slot is pending. Record for S3b/S4; do not hold the commit.\n- **F2 (nit): `endpoint-stop!` ignores an `invoke-close!` failure.** `WsHandle.close!`\n  and `close-ended!` emit `:ws/error :ws/close-failure` when the host close throws;\n  `endpoint-stop!` does not. Bookkeeping is unaffected (terminal event and slot release\n  never depend on the callback, per D3), but the diagnostic asymmetry is worth a line in\n  S3b.\n- **F3 (nit): `::host-stopped` produces `:stop {:outcome ...}` without `:since`**\n  (`stopped` assocs onto a nil `:stop`). Fine as \"never initiated\", but consumers should\n  read `:outcome` via `get-in`, as the tests do.\n- **F4 (robustness nit): an explicit `{:stop-grace-ms nil}` bounds override would make\n  `continue-stop`'s `(>= (- now since) nil)` throw.** Merging over `production-bounds`\n  protects the default; no layer validates this key. A `pos-int-or-nil?` guard or a\n  fallback to 0 would harden it.\n- **F5 (spec text, already moot): §2.1's `::invalid-table` definition (\"make-acceptor\n  throws\") does not match `make-acceptor`'s actual validation** — see deviation 2. The\n  architect may want the spec sentence corrected when S3a-2 lands.\n\n## 4. Regression surface\n\n- `ws_project.cljc`: the `adopt!` guard defaults to non-stopping (`:stopping?` absent on\n  every existing acceptor), and `:gone?` is write-only for existing consumers —\n  zero-breakage by construction, as §8 claims.\n- `ws.cljc`: `endpoint-stop!` is purely additive; `endpoint-step`/`accept-connection!`\n  untouched.\n- `head_ws_test.cljc`: fixture move is behavior-preserving except `unlisten!`'s new\n  `:stopped` deposit, which `head.ws` already handled (observe → `:status :stopped`).\n- No yin source, no core protocol file, no host adapter changed.\n\n## 5. Conditions of landing (owed at commit, per spec §8)\n\n1. Three-lane `bb test` green (JVM lane reported green by the engineer; Node and Dart\n   lanes were not run in this worktree — `npm ci` first). The new CLJD surface to watch:\n   `case` with a multi-constant list branch (`(:starting :serving)` in `serve-step`),\n   single-key `assoc` on nil in `stopped`, and the `#?(:cljd Object ...)` catch ordering\n   (already cljd-first in both places).\n2. `clj -M:kondo` clean over the changed and new files (engineer reports 0/0).\n3. Copy this report to\n   `collab/1791388000000-reviewer-stream-s3a-1.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**ACCEPT** — S3a-1 is spec-conformant, invariant-clean, and well-tested; the deviations\nare improvements. F1-F5 are recorded for S3b/S4 and do not block the S3a-1 commit, which\nremains conditional on the green three-lane run above.\n"}}]}}
