yin.head.ws/no-port` when the bind port is not\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-177-   a positive integer, since the descriptor every accepted handle\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-178-   reports names the port from the start; `:yin.head.ws/bind-failed`\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-179-   when `listen!` throws or answers an outcome that is not ok.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-180-  [{:keys [board principal bind-host bind-port listen!]}]\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-181-  (cond\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:182:    (not (loopback? bind-host))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:183:    (refused :yin.head.ws/not-loopback {:bind-host bind-host})\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-184-\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-185-    (not (and (integer? bind-port) (pos? bind-port)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-186-    (refused :yin.head.ws/no-port {:bind-port bind-port})\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc-187-"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_a9a6e57e82f24205960de4a1","content":[{"type":"text","text":"test/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:5:   The portable cases run over `dao.stream.loopback-net`, an in-process\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:17:            [dao.stream.loopback-net :refer [connect-on listen-on loopback-net pump! unlisten!]]\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:160:  (let [net (loopback-net)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:293:  (let [net (loopback-net)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:418:  (let [net (loopback-net)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:440:  (let [net (loopback-net)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:496:  (let [net (loopback-net)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:551:  (let [net (loopback-net)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc:573:  (let [net (loopback-net)"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"5e7ab21c-55b4-41d7-ae35-fdd4ed2bf87e"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"loopback-literal? was copied as-is from head.ws/loopback? — good (duplication remains until it's removed in S3a-2, but this is expected per deviation item #9).\n\nInteresting point: head_ws_test currently uses the shared net. However, the shared net's `unlisten!` also deposits `:stopped` (deviation item #8). In head_ws_test, the server (head.ws composition) reads the lifecycle medium — does head.ws's serve-step treat `:stopped` on the lifecycle as fatal? Let me check head.ws's lifecycle handling and the restart test. The engineer said \"the restart case still passes because that server simply becomes :stopped before being replaced.\" Let me quickly verify head.ws's serve-step handling.\n\nAlso check head_ws_test's real-socket JVM case — does it use unlisten!? If so, then ws/jvm.clj's `stop-listening!` etc.\n\nNext, check the documentation changes (dao.stream.remote.md, dao.stream.ws.md) — these have been modified. And verify the invariant \"no new core stream operations\" — remote_channel only uses stream/append!, next, cursor, close!, descriptor. No problems.\n\nFurther verification: remote-channel's `serve` — regarding the spec §2.1 requirement \"the server value includes `:stop` (`{:since now :outcome o}` after initiation), and on refusal `:reason` and details.\" In the refusal case, the :status key is... `refused` merges `{:status :refused :reason reason}` with data. In the bind-failed case during serve, data is {} — no details. In observe's bind-failed case, it's {:detail value}. No problem.\n\nAnother spec conformance check: §2.1 states \"serve validates bounds by passing them to make-endpoint, make-acceptor, and links.\" The engineer calls make-endpoint, make-acceptor, and links. ✓.\n\nBut an ordering issue: in `serve`, make-endpoint and make-acceptor run before `(link-policy b)`. If bounds are valid for endpoint/acceptor but the link policy is invalid, links will throw — before bind!. ✓ The test asserts that nothing is listening.\n\nWait — actually there's a subtle bug: `_ (link-policy b)` is called after make-acceptor. But make-acceptor could throw first for invalid bounds — that's still a composition error, so no problem.\n\nNext, potential real defects to consider:\n\nA. `serve-step` during `:starting`/`:serving` — drain-lifecycle first, then accept-step!. But the spec §4.3 order for the first stopping tick: accept-step! once, then close-sessions!+endpoint-stop!, then unbind!. In `begin-stop`, after accept-step! there's release! — but release! calls ws-project/stop! again (idempotent swap). No problem.\n\nB. `begin-stop`: if `unbind!` returns false because `:unbind!` doesn't exist on host ((fn? f) is false → returns false) → stopped ::unbind-failed. But a host without :unbind! is permitted (\"any of the three may be missing\")! §2.1 host ::= {:connect! f :bind! g :unbind! h}, any may be missing. If unbind! is absent, stop completes immediately as :stopped with outcome ::unbind-failed. Hmm — is this right? Spec §4.3: \"3. unbind! with the server's deposit!; throws or non-ok response records :stop {:outcome ::unbind-failed}, server is immediately :stopped.\" If unbind! is missing... nothing to release; arguably it should become :stopped unconfirmed, or immediately confirmed. Marking it as \"unbind-failed\" when the host simply doesn't have unbind! could be called a mislabel, but the consequence (immediately :stopped) is safe. Wait — but is that really so? If the host has no :unbind!, the listener stays alive (net still routes accept! to the endpoint). The server is :stopped but the listener is still bound and accept-connection! still accepts! Spec §4.1: \"subsequent accept-connection! continues to function unless the host listener is gone — that's the host's unbind!\". If unbind! is absent, subsequent connections would be accepted into pending slots and then... endpoint-stop! is only called during release! in the stopping tick; after :stopped, serve-step returns the server unchanged (case's default branch). So pending connections leak — accepted but never closed (unless the host itself times out, or the expiry inside endpoint-step... which never runs). Hmm. But wait: in that case, do offers reach the acceptor? accept-step! never runs after :stopped. Slots stay :pending until expiry-ms — but nothing steps the endpoint. Therefore, hosts lacking :unbind! leak live sockets after stop.\n\nHow realistic is this? yin.repl.host/websocket — do all three hosts provide unbind!? Let me check. JVM: dao.stream.ws.jvm's stop-listening!; Node: stop-listening!. yin.repl.host presumably wires :unbind! for JVM/Node. If unbind! always exists in real assemblies, the edge case is only for test hosts. Still, labeling \"no unbind! on host\" as ::unbind-failed and immediately marking :stopped is defensible (nothing more can be done), but leaking accepted pending connections is a defect worth pointing out. Actually wait — in begin-stop, release! ran endpoint-stop! before unbind!. After :stopped, a new accept-connection! (listener still alive) creates a new pending slot; nothing closes it. But this only happens when :unbind! is absent. Worth noting.\n\nActually let me re-check: `unbind!` returns `(and (fn? f) (try ...))` — f missing → false → stopped ::unbind-failed. The test `stop-while-starting-and-stop-twice-are-idempotent` uses a quiet bind host but keeps :unbind! from host-of. Hmm, `stop-completes-without-a-close-callback` overrides :unbind! with one that returns ok without depositing → unconfirmed after grace period. OK.\n\nC. `continue-stop` runs accept-step! every tick — which includes reap-sessions! and per-session mirror. Sessions were closed on the first tick and reaped at the end of the first tick? In begin-stop: accept-step! (reaps nothing yet — sessions aren't closed yet), release! closes sessions. Then the next tick's continue-stop: accept-step! → reaps the closed ones. The test asserts sessions are empty after the second tick. ✓\n\nD. Race between dial-step's :resolving and a server that has already refused? Irrelevant.\n\nE. `dial`'s events: `(when events {...})` — if the caller passes events as nil, the key is omitted. No problem.\n\nF. `close!` on dial during :resolving with a channel established but never attached: closes the ws handle. ✓ Also closes the traffic ring? No — traffic medium and ring remain open. Spec §4.4 doesn't require it. The dial's ring/traffic are fresh per dial and discarded. OK.\n\nG. `channel-lost?` on dial — after close!, the status is :closed and dial-step doesn't step. ✓\n\nH. endpoint-stop!: what about slots where `(:handle-state slot)` is nil? Only :pending slots have handle-state. ✓ Also `terminal!` emits :ws/closed via deposit! — deposit! appends to the control medium; if append fails (control full), deposit! closes... already closed. OK.\n\nI. `stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced` test — asserts `(is (every? :closed? (vals (rc/sessions @(:server w)))) \"sessions closed\")` after the first stopping tick. close-sessions! marks :closed?. ✓\n\nJ. Invariants: \"no ambient scheduler\" — none. \"No new core stream operations\" — endpoint-stop!/stop!/close-sessions! are composition operations on existing handles, not core stream protocol operations. ✓ Transport neutrality: remote-channel is under dao.stream and speaks only ws. ✓ The yin namespace is unchanged except for tests. Verify: git status shows only changes in dao/stream sources + test + docs + head_ws_test. src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc unchanged. ✓ (Status shows modified: docs, ws.cljc, ws_project.cljc, ws_project_test, ws_test, head_ws_test; new: remote_channel.cljc, loopback_net.cljc, remote_channel_test.cljc.)\n\nK. head_ws_test with the moved net: the net's `unlisten!` currently deposits `:stopped`. head.ws's serve-step — how does it handle a lifecycle `:stopped` fact? Let me check head.ws's lifecycle handling to see whether an unexpected :stopped deposition breaks a head.ws case (regression risk from deviation item #8). Also net's `close-conn!`: in the original head_ws fixture, did close-conn! call the server's closed! before the client's? If the order changed, could that break a head_ws test? Let me diff mentally — I can't diff, but I'll check head.ws's lifecycle handling.\n\nL. Another check: remote-channel's `serve` passes `:control (writer-target (buffer capacity))` and `:control-admission (admission capacity)` — the admission's value-domain is :portable-values. The control medium carries :ws/closed events with attachment id (string) and host handles? Offers carry host handles via slot offer media with handoff-admission :host-values. Control events: `{:ws/attachment id :ws/event ...}` — portable values. But wait, ws/accept-connection!'s pending handle emits events on the control medium with only portable keys. ✓ head.ws presumably did the same thing.\n\nM. `make-slots` — offer/ack buffers with capacity 1, cursors issued at anchor-newest. Slot admission: handoff-admission {:retention :evict-oldest :capacity 1 :value-domain :host-values}. ws_test's handoff-slot uses the same shape. ✓\n\nN. Check spec: server value keys — `:spec`, `:descriptor`, `:endpoint`, `:acceptor`, `:listener`, `:lifecycle`, `:lifecycle-cursor`, `:lifecycle-gaps`, `:stop`, `:reason`. The implementation also has `:host`, `:bounds`, `:deposit!`. Additional items are fine (tests use :deposit! for the lifecycle-gap case — needed to inject facts; the spec's own tests say \"push 70 :listener-error facts through deposit!,\" so exposing deposit! in the value is intended).\n\nO. Spec §2.1's dial value keys: `{:status :spec :descriptor :name :dial :handle :identity :outcome}` — implementation matches.\n\nP. dial-step spec: \":attached: when the channel's projection is closed (ws-project/closed? of (:project (ws-project/channel dial))) → :lost\" plus the engineer's :gone?. ✓\n\nQ. resolve-step: on ok → dial-reflect! of `(:dao.stream/descriptor r)`; spec says \"ok → dial-reflect! of the returned descriptor → :attached with :handle, :identity.\" ✓ Note: `:identity (:dao.stream/identity r)` — from the resolve outcome. ✓\n\nR. Hmm, in resolve-step if dial-reflect! fails → :lost with outcome a. Spec doesn't state explicitly; reasonable.\n\nS. Potential defect: in `dial-step`, if status is :resolving and the channel has vanished (server refused mid-resolve), resolve-step gets transport-error channel-gone without retry? → :lost. ✓\n\nT. `run-until` in tests with dt=0 — no problem.\n\nU. Test `a-lifecycle-gap-while-starting-is-terminal` — quiet's bind wraps deposit! to swallow :bind-succeeded. But `listen-on` has already registered the listener with the wrapped deposit! — unlisten etc. No problem. After refusal, asserts unbind! was called and slots were released. Note: refusal path — `lifecycle-lost` during :starting calls `(unbind! server)` — but wait, at that moment the server's `:listener` is set (bind succeeded). ✓ endpoint-stop! closes the pending dial connection. ✓\n\nV. Documentation: verify changes in dao.stream.remote.md §3.0/§3.1 and dao.stream.ws.md. Let me read the relevant sections.\n\nW. Also check whether ws.cljc's `closed!` function handles host close callbacks → terminal. And check `head.ws`'s serve-step lifecycle handling for :stopped (regression check for deviation item #8 on head_ws_test's restart case). The restart case: the server's port is unlistened, then a new serve on the same port. In head.ws, when the lifecycle receives `:stopped`... if head.ws treats an unexpected :stopped as fatal → the server becomes stopped; then the new serve replaces it. The engineer said it passes. But also the real-socket JVM case: does it use net's `unlisten!`? No — real sockets use ws/jvm's stop-listening!. No problem.\n\nLet me check head.ws's serve-step/lifecycle handling.\n\nThe fixture relocation touches `head_ws_test`, so let's check how `head.ws` handles the lifecycle `:stopped` fact (deviation #8), and also check the documentation changes.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_e24c39ec011d40b185ee7dad","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":":stopped|lifecycle|serve-step","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"head_limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_85baf4a8214541d0a6a6aa99","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"Explicit stop|Lifecycle observation|remote-channel|endpoint-stop","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/docs/design","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"head_limit":40}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791393306846,"messageId":"d742880f-9764-49b4-b6fb-8d2b07c7b4bd"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_e24c39ec011d40b185ee7dad","content":[{"type":"text","text":"src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:13:     not a loopback literal composes no endpoint.  `serve-step` drives\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:193:          lifecycle (buffer capacity)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:216:          lifecycle-cursor (mint lifecycle stream/anchor-newest)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:218:                     (stream/append! lifecycle {:kind kind :value value}))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:242:         :lifecycle lifecycle\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:243:         :lifecycle-cursor lifecycle-cursor}))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:247:  \"Apply one lifecycle fact the listener deposited.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:256:    :stopped\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:257:    (assoc server :status :stopped)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:262:(defn serve-step\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:264:   lifecycle facts first (a bind that fails asynchronously becomes the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:274:                   (let [r (stream/next (:lifecycle server)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:275:                                        (:lifecycle-cursor server))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc:277:                       (let [server (observe (assoc server :lifecycle-cursor"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_85baf4a8214541d0a6a6aa99","content":[{"type":"text","text":"docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:513:- **Explicit stop**: an accepting composition's `stop!` marks it stopping,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:516:  reaping; the ws endpoint's `endpoint-stop!` closes every connection still\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:524:- **Lifecycle observation**: the listener's lifecycle medium is a bounded\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:563:`dao.stream.remote-channel` is the stepped composition over this channel:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:345:  `endpoint-stop!` is that stop for the connections still pending: each is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:346:  closed with 1001 `dao.stream/endpoint-stopped`, its terminal `:ws/closed`\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:783:  `dao.stream.remote` (`dao.stream.remote-channel`). Probing an idle"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"50c2f4dd-dace-4e63-8b20-460a8662a2aa"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The documentation has been updated as required by §6. I'll read through all the sections of the revised documentation and check their contents.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_3da9fbd39500400c8e4d95e5","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md","offset":500,"limit":75}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_2f8f6eb5819f48a59ecd664e","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md","offset":330,"limit":35}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791393319275,"messageId":"0a8d5af6-370a-428d-8404-89e5ebc21dac"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_3da9fbd39500400c8e4d95e5","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 75 lines\nNote: showing lines 500-574. More of the file remains. Use offset=575 to continue.\n500:   retained per link. A dialing composition passes them to its link with the\n501:   rest of its `:dao.stream.remote/*` policy.\n502: - **Request liveness (`give-up-after`)**: `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after`,\n503:   a positive integer of milliseconds or nil, bounds how long a request may\n504:   stay unanswered, measured against the `now` a driver hands the link's\n505:   `:step` (2.4, Expiry); past it the channel is lost. A dialing composition\n506:   passes it to its link, and `(dial-step! dial now)` steps the link at\n507:   `now` and closes the ws handle on loss; the 1-arity `dial-step!` hands no\n508:   `now` and never expires.\n509: - **Session failure isolation**: An unhandled exception or malformed payload\n510:   in one session's projection or mirror step is isolated and caught; its\n511:   resources are closed and marked for reaping without crashing the acceptor\n512:   or affecting other healthy sessions.\n513: - **Explicit stop**: an accepting composition's `stop!` marks it stopping,\n514:   after which every offer is rejected as at the session cap;\n515:   `close-sessions!` closes every session's handle and ring and marks it for\n516:   reaping; the ws endpoint's `endpoint-stop!` closes every connection still\n517:   pending acknowledgement (`dao.stream.ws.md` Serving). A serving\n518:   composition stops in that order, driver-paced: stop initiates and does no\n519:   I/O; the next step runs one last bounded answering pass with offers\n520:   rejected, closes the sessions, then the pending connections, then asks\n521:   the host to release its listener. Completion is the host's `stopped`\n522:   fact or, failing a callback, a composed grace measured against the\n523:   driver's `now`; release bookkeeping never depends on the callback.\n524: - **Lifecycle observation**: the listener's lifecycle medium is a bounded\n525:   ring written only by the host. While starting, `bind-succeeded` serves\n526:   and `bind-failed` refuses, and a gap or end refuses as lifecycle-lost\n527:   (a lost `bind-failed` cannot be told from a lost `bind-succeeded`), with\n528:   a best-effort release of the listener and the pending connections. While\n529:   serving, diagnostics (`listener-error`, `upgrade-failed`) are kept, the\n530:   last few; a gap adopts the recovery cursor and is counted, since only\n531:   diagnostics were lost; `stopped` without a stop, or an end, is a\n532:   host-stopped stop after closing sessions and pending connections. While\n533:   stopping, `stopped` confirms the stop, and a gap or end completes it\n534:   unconfirmed. A channel drop on either side is never a source gap.\n535: \n536: ### 3.1 WebSocket\n537: \n538: `dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n539: a channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n540: attachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n541: `{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n542: wired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n543: `{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n544: (Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n545: handoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n546: vocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget `:ws/max-frame-bytes`\n547: (nil for unbounded), with the pending and outbound bounds of\n548: `dao.stream.ws.md` (Serving, Deposit Admission) beside it. An outbound\n549: `full` from the writer is the transient refusal the mirror rewinds on (2.3).\n550: \n551: **New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n552: answer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n553: the ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n554: the composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n555: `ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n556: keeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n557: `:ws/value` of each `:ws/payload` onto the ring buffer, and drops\n558: `:ws/error` diagnostics. A terminal lifecycle event for the attachment\n559: (`:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`) or a failure resolution (`:ws/not-found`,\n560: `:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n561: loss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n562: composition drives at its own cadence, as it drives `endpoint-step`.\n563: `dao.stream.remote-channel` is the stepped composition over this channel:\n564: `serve`/`serve-step`/`stop!` and `dial`/`dial-step`/`close!` over a\n565: portable endpoint specification it formats into the ws descriptor itself,\n566: with its `production-bounds` profile as the composition data of 3.0.\n567: \n568: Direction is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n569: acceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n570: nothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n571: cljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n572: `src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n573: cljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n574: "}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_2f8f6eb5819f48a59ecd664e","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 35 lines\nNote: showing lines 330-364. More of the file remains. Use offset=365 to continue.\n330: `maxPayload` on Node's `ws`). Where a host cannot send the code, its seam\n331: substitutes a supported teardown (Host wire codes, below).\n332: \n333: Ownership remains explicit in every failure case:\n334: \n335: - if the offer deposit is non-`ok`, the endpoint closes the pending\n336:   connection;\n337: - before acknowledgement, the endpoint retains the handle and can close it\n338:   on peer loss, endpoint stop, or the configured admission expiry; teardown\n339:   releases the slot, emits the one applicable terminal lifecycle event, and\n340:   makes any later acknowledgement stale;\n341: - after reading the offer, the composition also holds the handle and closes\n342:   it if acknowledgement cannot be deposited;\n343: - after acknowledgement, the composition owns the session handle, while the\n344:   endpoint continues to own its host connection for endpoint-wide stop;\n345:   `endpoint-stop!` is that stop for the connections still pending: each is\n346:   closed with 1001 `dao.stream/endpoint-stopped`, its terminal `:ws/closed`\n347:   deposited on the control medium and its slot released. It is idempotent,\n348:   and releasing the host listener stays the host's `unbind!`;\n349: - any non-`ok` traffic deposit after acceptance tears down the connection\n350:   under Deposit Admission.\n351: \n352: No callback reaches the composition. Offer and acknowledgement are ordinary\n353: stream values, and the composition-owned driver invokes `endpoint-step`\n354: without waiting or installing a waiter.\n355: \n356: **That stream is not the served stream.** A client's `append!` is a request to\n357: the server, never a broadcast: what a client writes lands in a medium only the\n358: serving host's own interpreters read. Nothing a client sends reaches the other\n359: attachments by default.\n360: \n361: The alternative — depositing a client's messages straight into the served\n362: stream — would make every client a writer to the stream every other client is\n363: reading, by nothing more than holding a handle. A composition that wants that\n364: writes it explicitly: an interpreter reads the request medium, decides what"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"c6173cf3-59f1-412e-8dc7-a2f2972807fc"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The documentation appears consistent with the spec §6 modifications regarding S3a-1 (remote.md §3.0/§3.1, ws.md Serving). Please check the rewrite of the Liveness item under \"Deferred\" in ws.md (around line 783).","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_fec0ade3185f4dc896e56c1e","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md","offset":770,"limit":30}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791393353676,"messageId":"7b2d7d23-6939-40db-982c-ae547b5d2b62"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_fec0ade3185f4dc896e56c1e","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 30 lines\nNote: showing lines 770-799. More of the file remains. Use offset=800 to continue.\n770:   handle to bound a peer that never completes the handshake. `yin.repl`\n771:   is this gap's first consumer and `dao.jing.remote` (deprecated by\n772:   `dao.stream.remote.md`) its second, and neither can close it from where\n773:   it stands.\n774: - Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).\n775: - Liveness: for a connection used as a `dao.stream.remote.md` channel,\n776:   request liveness is decided there (2.4, Expiry): a request unanswered\n777:   past `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after`, against the `now` a driver hands\n778:   the link's step, loses the channel, and the dial closes this handle.\n779:   The idle probe is the consumer's own periodic read and its deadline: a\n780:   reader that polls sends a `next` the mirror answers, and an unanswered\n781:   one expires; its cadence is the composition's. No protocol ping/pong is\n782:   composed, and the open question is closed for channels used through\n783:   `dao.stream.remote` (`dao.stream.remote-channel`). Probing an idle\n784:   connection used outside a remote channel remains open.\n785: - Resumption protocol: stream identity is stable across reconnects, so\n786:   resumption is possible by design; what history a rejoining dialer receives\n787:   (and how it states where it left off) is not yet specified for the copy\n788:   path. Through `dao.stream.remote.md` a kept cursor is the whole answer.\n789: - Backpressure protocols above the stream (a reader publishing its progress\n790:   as data is interpretation, outside both this transport and the contract).\n791:   Needing nothing from this transport does not mean needing no specification:\n792:   a pause vocabulary both ends must recognize is interoperable wire content,\n793:   and it requires a durable home before it is built. `dao.lease.md` is the\n794:   intended home for its semantics and does not yet claim them; the wire form\n795:   is owed by neither that document nor this one until it does. The v2\n796:   migration plan has deferred flow control out of its slice for the same\n797:   reason, so nothing is being built against an unsettled vocabulary.\n798: - Two host seams (http-kit server, Dart) expose no outbound backlog after\n799:   establishment. There `:ws/outbound-high-water` does not apply, `append!`"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"094924c3-182c-4bb8-8da2-68f34e81cd7e"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Documentation fixes are reflected. Now, some remaining verification items:\n\n1. Check the restart case in head_ws_test.cljc and the real-socket JVM case — confirm that moving the fixtures hasn't changed semantics. In particular, `unlisten!` deposits `:stopped`, and the behavior of head.ws's `observe` on `:stopped`: lines 256-257 `(assoc server :status :stopped)` — so head.ws's server becomes :stopped. In the restart test, the old server is simply replaced. And head.ws's serve-step at :stopped — need to confirm it doesn't break. According to the engineer, tests pass. Can't run tests in plan mode (shell is blocked). This is a review-level constraint worth noting — actually, running tests is nearly read-only but is blocked. Note it.\n\nWait — actually there's one potential regression from deviation #8: in head_ws_test, is there any case that calls `unlisten!` and then continues to step the old server expecting :serving? Example: dht restart flow. The engineer states only the restart case uses it. head.ws's observe maps :stopped → :status :stopped, and serve-step presumably returns stopped servers as-is. The test then creates a new server afterward. Claimed to pass.\n\n2. Confirm the use of `closed!` in loopback-net: `close-conn!` calls the server-side closed! and then the client-side closed!. The original head_ws fixture may have been in the same order. No problem.\n\n3. Check remote_channel_test's `stop-while-starting-and-stop-twice`: quiet's bind doesn't deposit bind-succeeded and returns an ok map with :port. Server is :starting. stop! → :stopping. First serve-step 0: begin-stop → accept-step!, release!, since=0, unbind! → net/unbind-on... wait, the host has :unbind! (counting? no — the host is host-of with only bind! changed). unbind-on deposits :stopped on pump and dissocs the listener by (:port listener) — listener is {:dao.stream/outcome :ok :port (:bind-port opts)} → dissocs port 9. OK. Then serve-step 1: continue-stop → drain-lifecycle finds :stopped → confirmed. ✓ Test pumps before serve-step 1. ✓\n\n4. Another potential defect: `begin-stop` sets `:stop :since now` before unbind!. If unbind fails → stopped with ::unbind-failed but :since is set. No problem.\n\n5. `continue-stop` grace check: `(>= (- now since) stop-grace-ms)`. Test: since=1000, grace=100; serve-step 1099 → 99 < 100 → still stopping; at 1100 → 100 ≥ 100 → stopped. ✓ Note: continue-stop at 1099 also does accept-step! and endpoint-stop! — harmless.\n\n6. Edge: `drain-lifecycle` inside continue-stop can transition to :stopped :confirmed before the grace check — guarded by `(= :stopping (:status server))`. ✓\n\n7. `observe`'s catch-all `(= :stopped kind)` — what about a :stopped fact arriving during :refused or :stopped state? drain-lifecycle only runs while status is live (contains? live). Once refused/stopped, no further draining. ✓ But note: the drain-lifecycle loop checks `(contains? live (:status server))` before reading the next fact — once status becomes :stopped mid-drain, the remaining facts stay in the medium. No problem.\n\n8. `sessions` — `(some-> (:acceptor server) ws-project/sessions)`. For refused server (select-keys only [:spec :descriptor]) → nil. ✓\n\n9. Missing :lifecycle/:lifecycle-cursor keys on refused-from-bind-failed server (select-keys drops them). If serve-step is called on the refused server, it returns as-is (default of case). ✓\n\n10. `descriptor-of` with nil path → identity \"ws://h:p\" + nil → does (str nil) become \"\"? (str \"ws://\" \"h\" \":\" 9 nil) → \"ws://h:9\". ws/descriptor? may require path to be a string. Does serve validate path? No, there's no path validation — a spec without :path produces a descriptor with :ws/path nil. Does make-endpoint validate the descriptor? Need to check the requirements of descriptor? — canonical-path? Let me check ws/descriptor?.\n\nLet me read lines 50-130 of ws.cljc to see descriptor?/canonical-path?/checked-bounds and make-endpoint.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Let me verify `make-endpoint`'s descriptor validation (does `serve` catch a spec with no `:path`?).\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_1279e1d423ff4d578754d30e","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc","offset":50,"limit":90}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791393388209,"messageId":"a56f3a96-533b-44f5-b3f7-9ced3a925078"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_1279e1d423ff4d578754d30e","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 90 lines\nNote: showing lines 50-139. More of the file remains. Use offset=140 to continue.\n50: (defn- outcome\n51:   [x]\n52:   {:dao.stream/outcome x})\n53: \n54: \n55: (defn canonical-path?\n56:   \"True for the already-canonical request-target form carried on a descriptor.\n57:    URI parsing and bind policy belong to the host adapter; this gate prevents a\n58:    query, fragment, relative path, or unresolved dot segment from reaching the\n59:    exact served-path lookup.\"\n60:   [path]\n61:   (and (string? path)\n62:        (not (empty? path))\n63:        (str/starts-with? path \"/\")\n64:        (not (str/includes? path \"?\"))\n65:        (not (str/includes? path \"#\"))\n66:        (not (some #{\".\" \"..\"} (str/split path #\"/\")))))\n67: \n68: \n69: (defn descriptor?\n70:   \"The settled WebSocket descriptor gate.  It names a served stream, not a\n71:    particular connection, and contains only portable reachability data.\n72:    The one-argument form keeps the Transit domain as the public default; a\n73:    composition selecting a codec profile gates the same descriptor against\n74:    that profile's domain.\"\n75:   ([x] (descriptor? x transit/profile))\n76:   ([x codec]\n77:    (and (map? x)\n78:         (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type x))\n79:         (string? (:dao.stream/identity x))\n80:         (string? (:ws/host x))\n81:         (integer? (:ws/port x))\n82:         (pos? (:ws/port x))\n83:         (canonical-path? (:ws/path x))\n84:         ((:ws/portable-value? codec) x))))\n85: \n86: \n87: (defn admission?\n88:   \"Assembly-time declaration required for every transport deposit medium.\"\n89:   [x]\n90:   (and (map? x)\n91:        (= :evict-oldest (:retention x))\n92:        (integer? (:capacity x))\n93:        (pos? (:capacity x))\n94:        (contains? #{:host-values :portable-values} (:value-domain x))))\n95: \n96: \n97: (defn- writer-target?\n98:   [x]\n99:   (and (map? x)\n100:        (stream/writer? (:dao.stream/handle x))\n101:        (contains? (:dao.stream/surface x) :writer)))\n102: \n103: \n104: (defn- checked-target\n105:   [target declaration]\n106:   (when-not (and (writer-target? target) (admission? declaration))\n107:     (throw (ex-info \"invalid DaoStream WebSocket deposit composition\"\n108:                     {:target target :admission declaration})))\n109:   target)\n110: \n111: \n112: (defn- attachment-id\n113:   []\n114:   (str (random-uuid)))\n115: \n116: \n117: (def ^:private bound-keys\n118:   \"The byte and frame bounds a composition may set on `make-attacher` and\n119:    `make-endpoint`.  Each is a positive integer or nil; nil is unbounded.\"\n120:   [:ws/max-frame-bytes :ws/max-pending-frames :ws/max-pending-bytes\n121:    :ws/outbound-high-water :ws/max-outbound-bytes])\n122: \n123: \n124: (defn- checked-bounds\n125:   [config]\n126:   (let [bounds (select-keys config bound-keys)]\n127:     (when-not (every? (fn [[_ v]] (or (nil? v) (and (integer? v) (pos? v)))) bounds)\n128:       (throw (ex-info \"invalid DaoStream WebSocket bounds\" {:bounds bounds})))\n129:     bounds))\n130: \n131: \n132: (defn- payload-size\n133:   \"The raw size of one wire payload: characters for a text payload, bytes\n134:    for host bytes.  `:cljd` comes first because the cljd host-eval pass also\n135:    reads a `:clj` branch.\"\n136:   [payload]\n137:   (if (string? payload)\n138:     (count payload)\n139:     #?(:cljd (.-length ^List payload)"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"6f2c8813-5ec2-4a63-8163-5e3dd8fe4161"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Check make-endpoint — is it validating the descriptor via descriptor?? Read lines 492–580.\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_56b1430ce64b466098c6bc53","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s3a/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc","offset":492,"limit":86}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791393397457,"messageId":"1015cec5-bdd9-4793-8dac-3fdc30f3f3c6"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_56b1430ce64b466098c6bc53","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 86 lines\nNote: showing lines 492-577. More of the file remains. Use offset=578 to continue.\n492: (defn make-attacher\n493:   \"Construct the host-composed unary `attach!` entry point.\n494: \n495:    `:connect!` receives the descriptor and the private adapter map, starts\n496:    connection establishment, and synchronously returns a raw-socket adapter\n497:    containing `:send!` and `:close!`.  It must not wait for the peer.\n498: \n499:    `:codec` selects the wire profile explicitly (default: Transit).  The\n500:    offer is exactly the selected subprotocol, so a peer that does not speak\n501:    it fails the handshake rather than being downgraded.\n502: \n503:    The byte and frame bounds (`:ws/max-frame-bytes`, `:ws/max-pending-frames`,\n504:    `:ws/max-pending-bytes`, `:ws/outbound-high-water`,\n505:    `:ws/max-outbound-bytes`) are optional positive integers; nil is\n506:    unbounded.  The seam may carry `:queued-bytes`, a no-argument function\n507:    answering the host's outbound backlog; without it the outbound bound is a\n508:    cumulative quota of encoded bytes sent since open.\"\n509:   [{:keys [traffic admission connect! codec] :as config}]\n510:   (let [target (checked-target traffic admission)\n511:         codec (checked-codec codec)\n512:         bounds (checked-bounds config)]\n513:     (when-not (fn? connect!)\n514:       (throw (ex-info \"WebSocket host composition requires :connect!\" {:config config})))\n515:     (fn attach!\n516:       [descriptor]\n517:       (if-not (descriptor? descriptor codec)\n518:         (outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor)\n519:         (let [id (attachment-id)\n520:               [handle state] (new-handle descriptor id target :connecting nil codec bounds)]\n521:           (try\n522:             (let [socket (connect! descriptor (adapter handle))]\n523:               (if (and (map? socket) (fn? (:send! socket)) (fn? (:close! socket)))\n524:                 (do (swap! state assoc :socket socket)\n525:                     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n526:                      :dao.stream/handle handle\n527:                      :dao.stream/attachment id})\n528:                 (outcome :dao.stream/transport-error)))\n529:             (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n530:               (outcome :dao.stream/transport-error))))))))\n531: \n532: \n533: ;; Server acceptance is intentionally an endpoint composition object, not a\n534: ;; global transport directory.  Its slots are supplied and owned by the host.\n535: ;; One descriptor, not a served-path table: the mirror answers not-found\n536: ;; per identity now, so this transport no longer routes an upgrade by path\n537: ;; at all -- every upgrade this endpoint's own codec negotiation accepts is\n538: ;; accepted, and what a client can reach through it is entirely the\n539: ;; mirror's own table.  The byte and frame bounds are those of\n540: ;; `make-attacher`, applied to every accepted connection; the pending bounds\n541: ;; are what keeps a peer from growing the pre-acknowledgement queue.\n542: (defn make-endpoint\n543:   [{:keys [descriptor control control-admission slots codecs] :as config}]\n544:   (let [control-target (checked-target control control-admission)\n545:         codecs (or codecs [transit/profile])\n546:         bounds (checked-bounds config)]\n547:     (when-not (seq slots)\n548:       (throw (ex-info \"WebSocket endpoint needs handoff slots\" {:config config})))\n549:     (when-not (and (seq codecs) (every? codec-profile? codecs))\n550:       (throw (ex-info \"WebSocket endpoint needs at least one codec profile\" {:codecs codecs})))\n551:     (when-not (apply distinct? (map :ws/subprotocol codecs))\n552:       (throw (ex-info \"WebSocket endpoint codec subprotocols must be distinct\" {:codecs codecs})))\n553:     (when-not (= :portable-values (:value-domain control-admission))\n554:       (throw (ex-info \"endpoint control medium must carry portable values\" {:admission control-admission})))\n555:     ;; The one descriptor crosses under every profile the endpoint speaks,\n556:     ;; or the profile that cannot carry it must not be offered.\n557:     (when-not (every? #(descriptor? descriptor %) codecs)\n558:       (throw (ex-info \"invalid served descriptor\" {:descriptor descriptor})))\n559:     (doseq [slot slots]\n560:       (checked-target (:offer slot) (:offer-admission slot))\n561:       (checked-target (:ack slot) (:ack-admission slot))\n562:       (when-not (and (= 1 (get-in slot [:offer-admission :capacity]))\n563:                      (= :host-values (get-in slot [:offer-admission :value-domain]))\n564:                      (= 1 (get-in slot [:ack-admission :capacity]))\n565:                      (= :host-values (get-in slot [:ack-admission :value-domain]))\n566:                      (some? (:ack-cursor slot)))\n567:         (throw (ex-info \"invalid capacity-one WebSocket handoff slot\" {:slot slot}))))\n568:     {:config config\n569:      :bounds bounds\n570:      :codecs codecs\n571:      :codec-index (zipmap (map :ws/subprotocol codecs) codecs)\n572:      :state (atom {:control control-target\n573:                    :slots (mapv (fn [slot]\n574:                                   (assoc slot :status :free)) slots)\n575:                    :connections {}})}))\n576: \n577: "}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"c3a2539a-89e1-4ad5-9b4c-fa852c36eec0"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`make-endpoint` validates the descriptor via `descriptor?`, which requires `canonical-path?` — so a spec without `:path` (nil) will throw from make-endpoint inside `serve` as a composition error. This isn't refusal-as-data. Spec §2.1 lists as refusals no-transport, no-port, bind-failed, lifecycle-lost, invalid-table — a malformed path isn't included in the refusal list, and \"composition errors from any of them are re-thrown as the composition errors they are, before anything is listened to.\" So throwing for a nil path is per spec. Hmm, but is that really the case? The spec validates `:port` as refusal data, but the descriptor gate handles the path. Consistent with the spec.\n\nBut wait — there's an asymmetry: `:port` is validated as refusal `::no-port` before make-endpoint, but the descriptor? inside make-endpoint also requires a pos-int port. No problem.\n\nAlso regarding `dial`: descriptor-of is computed, but does dial validate the descriptor? `ws-project/dial-resolve!` → establish! → attach! validates the descriptor → invalid-descriptor result → resolve-step... no wait: establish! returns r (attach's result), which is non-ok → dial-resolve! responds with that outcome → the cond in resolve-step: transport-error? invalid-descriptor isn't transport-error → :else → :lost with outcome invalid-descriptor. Hmm, actually `(or (when (nil? @channel) (establish! dial cd)) ((:resolve! @channel) ...))` — establish! returns non-nil r on failure, so dial-resolve! answers with it. Then dial-step: status :lost, outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}. Reasonable.\n\nNow, remaining checks:\n- `ask!` on a test whose ring stays open (a-lost-close-event...) — no problem.\n- remote-channel's `dial` composes `:cursor (mint traffic stream/anchor-newest)` before attach! — required per ws-project/dial's docs. ✓\n- In remote-channel/dial, `traffic` buffer capacity comes from bounds. The attacher's admission is `:portable-values` with the same capacity. head.ws probably did the same thing.\n\nNext, potential issues: **the test `a-lifecycle-gap-while-serving` asserts diagnostics count 8 and gaps 1.** Deposit 70 facts into a capacity-64 ring; the cursor is issued at anchor-newest at serve time. The server ticks once at 0 (bind-succeeded is read). Then 70 deposits → the ring holds the last 64; read from cursor → gap → recovery cursor → read remaining facts. drain-lifecycle: first next → gap → lifecycle-lost increments gaps and adopts the cursor; loop continues; reads up to 64 listener-error facts → diagnose keeps the last 8. ✓ But wait: the gap response's cursor — after adopting the recovery cursor, do the remaining facts include the evicted ones? No: the recovery cursor points to the oldest retained. 70 deposited, 64 retained (facts 6..69). Gap count 1. ✓ Also `tick!` at 1 runs serve-step → drain-lifecycle → then accept-step! (still serving). Then attach! at 2. ✓\n\n- `a-host-that-stops-under-us`: unlisten! closes conns (server-side closed! → deposits :ws/closed on each session's traffic; client's closed! → deposits on dial's traffic), then deposits :stopped on the lifecycle. serve-step 1: drain-lifecycle reads :stopped → observe: catchall `(= :stopped kind)` → release! + stopped ::host-stopped. Note: drain runs before accept-step!, and since the status left starting/serving, accept-step! is skipped. Sessions closed via release!. Test asserts session's :closed? and ring closure. ✓ Then dial-step → :lost. ✓ Note: release! also runs endpoint-stop!. No problem.\n\nBut subtle point: in observe, the :stopped catchall runs in any live status other than stopping... including :starting (deviation #5, documented). ✓\n\n- Next, `stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced`: after `stop!`, asserts `(stream/append! reader-ws :still-open)` → ok. Then serve-step 10 → begin-stop: accept-step! at now=10. Wait — the pending late connection: accept-step!'s first endpoint-step (release dead pendings — late conn is still alive), then adopt offers: the late offer is read from the slot's offer medium → adopt! rejects (stopping) → closes handle via stream/close! → WsHandle's close! → invoke-close! 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\" → net enqueues close-conn → (later on pump) both sides closed!. Also terminal? close! doesn't emit a terminal — the host's close callback (closed!) emits the terminal :ws/closed. In the net, close-conn! calls the server's adapter's closed! → ws adapter's closed! → handle's closed! → terminal! :ws/closed to control. That happens on pump. accept-step!'s second endpoint-step: slot is still :pending with phase :closed → release-slot!. ✓ Then per-session pass: reader session's mirror answers the queued next request (the :value appended to source before stop... wait, poll! happened before stop; the request frame was pumped to server; the session's projection+mirror at begin-stop's accept-step! answers it — the answer is written to reader-ws → enqueued to client). Then release!: close-sessions! closes reader's handle (1000 detached) and ring; endpoint-stop! — pending slot already released, nothing. unbind! → count 1, listener dissoc'd, :stopped deposit enqueued.\n\nThen test asserts slots all free ✓, sessions closed ✓. pump → client receives answer + close. dial-step 10 → projection reads :ws/payload answer, then :ws/closed → closes ring; link drains filed answers. poll! h c → :value ✓. dial :lost? dial-step: after ws-project/dial-step!, status is :attached, channel-lost? → projection closed → :lost ✓.\n\nThen serve-step 20: continue-stop → accept-step! reaps closed sessions → drain-lifecycle reads :stopped → confirmed ✓. Calls still 1 ✓.\n\nAll consistent.\n\nNow, adversarial findings to consider:\n\n**Finding 1 (minor/edge): host without `:unbind!` stops as `::unbind-failed`, leaving the listener bound and future pending connections unclosed.** After :stopped, serve-step is inert, so a host that keeps routing upgrades (because it couldn't be unbound) will accumulate pending connections that are never closed nor expired (endpoint-step never runs again). Spec §2.1 states that any of the three host functions may be absent. Options: treat absence of :unbind! as unconfirmed → stopped after grace, or continue endpoint-stop! while connections exist. Severity: low (real assemblies all provide unbind!; yin.repl.host documents :unbind!). Worth recording.\n\nActually wait — does it matter? If no unbind!, `unbind!` returns false → `stopped ::unbind-failed` — server reaches terminal immediately while listener is live. endpoint-stop! was run once in release!. New connections accepted after that stay :pending forever with a live socket. expiry-ms is set (15000) but endpoint-step never runs again. This is a resource leak at that edge. Also the outcome label is misleading. Flag as minor defect / non-blocking.\n\n**Finding 2 (minor): `endpoint-stop!` ignores failure of `invoke-close!`.** No `:ws/error :ws/close-failure` diagnostic like WsHandle's close!/close-ended! emits. If host's close! throws, failure is silent. Terminal event is still deposited, slot released — bookkeeping doesn't depend on callback (D3 ✓). Minor nit.\n\n**Finding 3: `stopped` helper on host-stopped produces `:stop` without `:since`.** Shape is `{:outcome ...}`. Spec says `:stop {:since now :outcome o}` \"once initiated\" — host-stopped isn't initiated, so acceptable; but consumers expecting :since may trip. Nit.\n\n**Finding 4: `observe`'s `:stopped` catchall runs during `:starting` too.** Deviation #5, documented, reasonable.\n\n**Finding 5: `serve`'s bind-failed path discards endpoint/acceptor without `endpoint-stop!`.** If `bind!` threw after partially registering (e.g., net's listen-on throws before registering on port conflict — no pending). Can pending connections exist at the moment bind! answers non-ok? Only if host started accepting during the bind! call and then reported failure. Extreme edge; spec doesn't require. Skip or nit.\n\n**Finding 6: `release!` on `:stopping` → host-stopped (via observe's :stopped during :stopping is handled by stopped :confirmed; via lifecycle-lost during :stopping → unconfirmed).** Wait: lifecycle-lost during :stopping just returns `(stopped server ::unconfirmed)` without release! — but release! already ran on the first stopping tick (begin-stop). Subsequent ticks of continue-stop keep endpoint-stop! running. OK.\n\n**Finding 7: dial's `:events` in remote-channel is passed as raw handle, but does remote's emit! expect a writer?** emit! does `(stream/append! w v)` — works with handle. And does head.ws pass the events ring similarly? Quick check — head/ws.cljc dial's events. Probably the same. No problem.\n\n**Finding 8: `valid-table?` is structural check only** (deviation #2). Spec said `::invalid-table` is \"if any table entry's cursor holds a host object, i.e., make-acceptor throws.\" Does make-acceptor validate table entry handles? make-acceptor only requires `(map? table)` — doesn't validate entries at all! So the spec's claim that \"make-acceptor throws\" was itself inaccurate. Engineer's structural check (`:handle` exists) is a reasonable substitute. But what about a table entry whose `:handle` is a host object (e.g., a Java object)? `some?` passes. Then mirror-step fails per session → isolated by accept-step!'s try/catch → session closed. Hmm, does mirror-step validate table entries? dao.stream.remote mirror requires table entries to be handles/cursors. Invalid entry may throw inside session step → isolated. Also, `:value-domain :portable-values` admission of traffic medium means host objects can't cross the wire anyway. Deviation acceptable; note that `valid-table?` doesn't check that `:handle` is a stream reader — mirror-step validation handles that. Non-blocking finding.\n\n**Finding 9: `::invalid-table` refusal response includes `:spec` but not descriptor — no problem.**\n\n**Finding 10: Test coverage gaps against spec §7's list.** Spec enumerates 15 remote_channel cases; engineer states 15 cases landed. Comparing names:\n1. serve-formats-the-descriptor-below-the-boundary ✓\n2. a-reader-resolves-and-attaches-over-the-stepped-composition ✓\n3. production-bounds-reach-every-layer ✓\n4. idle-healthy-board-versus-blackholed-request ✓\n5. unrelated-traffic-flood-cannot-defer-expiry ✓\n6. a-lost-close-event-is-recovered-by-the-deadline ✓\n7. stop-is-explicit-and-driver-paced ✓\n8. stop-completes-without-a-close-callback ✓\n9. stop-while-starting-and-stop-twice-are-idempotent ✓\n10. a-lifecycle-gap-while-serving-is-counted-and-serving-continues ✓\n11. a-lifecycle-gap-while-starting-is-terminal ✓\n12. a-host-that-stops-under-us-is-stopped-host-stopped ✓\n13. session-cap-and-idle-expiry-hold-under-the-profile ✓\n14. a-newcomer-during-stop-is-rejected ✓\n15. no-transport-and-no-port-are-refusals-as-data ✓\n\nSpec §7 also required ws_test case ✓ and two ws_project_test cases ✓. Note: spec's §7 for S3a-1 doesn't require head_board_test etc. (that's S3a-2).\n\nOne spec test detail: \"production-bounds-reach-every-layer: ... link policy (via next exceeding max-outstanding with a counting-writer-like seam, or by reading dial's :policy)\" — engineer reads :policy. Explicitly permitted. ✓\n\nAnother detail: spec's stop test says of pending connections \"both see 1001\"? No — that's the ws_test case; the remote-channel stop test follows deviation #3 not asserting 1001. Spec §7 says \"the reader's session handle and the pending handle are closed (both deposit :ws/closed), the slot frees, unbind! is called once.\" Test asserts conn closure, :ws/closed on control medium, slot free, unbind once. ✓ Deviation #3 justified — spec's own ordering (accept-step! before endpoint-stop!) means pending offers get rejected on adopt (1000), not endpoint-stop (1001). Actually wait — is that so? In begin-stop, first accept-step!: the late offer was already deposited on slot's offer medium. adopt! rejects and closes handle with generic close → 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\". Hmm, but is this the desired wire behavior for a connection rejected during stop? ws.md may assign 1001 \"going away\" semantics for server shutdown. 1000 for \"detached\" is a re-attachable normal closure — actually fits: the reader should re-attach. No problem.\n\nBut actually, hold on: does adopt!'s rejection close the handle before endpoint-step observes it? Order inside accept-step!: endpoint-step (releases dead pendings; late conn isn't dead yet), adopt loop reads offers → adopt! closes handle (phase :closed synchronously; net enqueues close callback), second endpoint-step → first doseq releases slots where handle-state phase is :closed → released. ✓ Test asserts slot free after serve-step 10 without pump — release is via phase check, not callback. ✓ Good: bookkeeping doesn't depend on callback.\n\n**Finding 11: `dial-step` at `:resolving` calls resolve-step once per tick.** Spec: \"one resolve per tick\". ✓\n\n**Finding 12: `channel-lost?` is checked only when `:attached`.** During :resolving, resolve outcome handles loss. ✓\n\n**Finding 13: In ws_project/dial-step!, `:gone?` set + handle close.** `(swap! channel assoc :gone? true)` — channel is an atom holding a map; no problem. But note: this runs only `(when-some [handle (:handle ch)])` — if ch is nil, stepped is nil, no-op. ✓ Also `stepped` is computed by `((:step! ch) (:cd ch) now)` — the link's step responds with a map including channel-gone?. In remote.cljc line 1128: step responds with `{:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? ...}`. ✓ And channel-loss! at line 711 clears outstanding and sets channel-gone? true.\n\nHmm, one question: if `(:step! ch)` is nil (channel not established), `stepped` is nil — `(when (:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? stepped))` on nil → nil. ✓\n\n**Finding 14: Does the addition of `:gone?` change ws_project/dial-step! behavior for existing consumers (head.ws)?** head.ws's dial-step calls ws-project/dial-step! — now the handle is also closed when a link expires. Previously (S2c), did dial-step! already close the handle on channel-gone? Docstring says \"if a request exceeds deadline, the channel is lost, and the dial that owns the connection closes the ws handle\" — was this there before, or is it new? Engineer states the only addition is recording `:gone? true`; close was already there (\"channel-loss!... dial-step! closes the ws handle\" is in spec §3.1 S2c). So the addition is only the flag + docstring. Existing head.ws consumers: head.ws's dial-step checks projection's closed? — with :gone?, head.ws doesn't read it; behavior unchanged aside from flag recording. Zero breakage ✓.\n\n**Finding 15: CLJD compatibility.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — engineer notes :cljd comes first elsewhere (payload-size comment states cljd first because cljd's host-eval reads :clj branches). In remote_channel.cljc line 322: `(catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ ...)` — :cljd first ✓. Line 355 same ✓. `case` on `(:status server)` with keyword constants — no problem. `mapv`, `select-keys`, `get-in` fine. `(assoc (:stop server) :outcome outcome)` — single-key assoc on nil map... CLJD's trap was \"multi-key assoc on nil\". Single-key assoc on nil is fine in Clojure; in CLJD? Probably fine. Hmm, worth flagging as unverified on Dart since Dart lane hasn't been run.\n\nAlso `doseq` with `map-indexed vector` — no problem. `take-last` no problem.\n\nOne cljd concern: `(def ^:private max-diagnostics 8)` no problem. `contains?` on sets/maps no problem.\n\n**Finding 16: `serve-step`'s case uses `(:starting :serving)` — a case with a list constant.** In Clojure's `case`, `(:starting :serving)` is treated as multiple test constants — valid. In CLJS, case with list of keywords compiles fine. CLJD? Engineer notes \"case on vector constant\" trap — this is a list and used elsewhere? head.ws's serve-step probably similar pattern. Quick check... actually remote_channel's `case (:status server)` with branches `(:starting :serving)`, `:stopping`, default `server`. CLJS's `case` supports multiple constants in a list. Should be fine in CLJD too, but unverified (Dart lane not run). Flag as verification debt.\n\n**Finding 17: `refused` at `::bind-failed` in serve drops `:host`, `:bounds`, `:deposit!`, `:lifecycle`** — so a bind-failed refusal can't drain lifecycle facts (e.g., an async :bind-failed arrives later). No problem — terminal.\n\nBut wait: async bind-failed during :starting is handled in observe. Synchronous non-ok from bind! → refuse immediately. ✓ Spec: \"if bind! throws, responds non-ok, or deposits :bind-failed\" — deposited case is handled by observe (test a-lifecycle... no, is there a test for bind-failed fact? Spec §7 doesn't enumerate; no problem).\n\n**Finding 18: `stop!` on a server whose bind! synchronously deposited facts but never ticked** — no problem.\n\n**Finding 19: `drain-lifecycle`'s default branch** — `case` with no match → `server` (the trailing expression acts as default). If outcome is blocked/other, returns server and loop exits? No — `case` responds with server, then `recur`? Let me look: `(let [r (stream/next ...)] (case (:dao.stream/outcome r) :ok (recur ...) :gap (recur ...) :end (lifecycle-lost server true) server))` — default responds with server, loop ends. ✓ blocked → exits loop. ✓\n\n**Finding 20: Test `production-bounds-reach-every-layer` composes server and dial with production bounds** — serve with production bounds calls bind! (listen-on) → listener registered on port 9 of new net. Also asserts `(empty? (:listeners @lnet))` only for the invalid bounds net. ✓\n\n**Finding 21: net's `flood!` delivers directly to client adapter, bypassing conn's closed? check?** It checks `(not @(:closed? conn))`. Delivers junk :ws/value frames to client → adapter's message! → handle's receive! → deposits :ws/payload on traffic → projection appends to ring. drain-budget 4 limits per-tick link drain. Test asserts expiry at deadline tick despite flood. ✓ Matches spec §7.\n\nNote: flood frames use attachment... `(transit/encode {:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value {:junk i}})` — delivered to client adapter which deposits with its own attachment. Ring capacity 64 (default) — junk appends may evict real answers. That's the point: even a flood that evicts doesn't defer expiry. ✓\n\n**Finding 22: `a-lost-close-event-is-recovered-by-the-deadline` manually appends directly to traffic with attachment \"other\"** — projection ignores other attachments but cursor... wait, traffic is dial's medium with capacity 8; the real :ws/closed for the dial's attachment was evicted by 8 fake events. Projection step: reads events, ignores \"other\" attachment, no :ws/closed → ring